// These ten lessons are intentionally public. No Pro lesson content belongs here.
const freeLessons = {
  'python-automation': [
    {
      objectives: ['Run a Python file from a terminal.', 'Recognize variables, strings, and numeric calculations.', 'Describe a task in terms of inputs, transformations, and outputs.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Start with a small, repeatable task', text: 'Automation works best when the rules are clear. Imagine that every Friday you calculate the total for three invoices. The input is a set of amounts, the transformation is addition, and the output is a labeled total. Start with this small example before connecting real files or accounts. A program executes instructions exactly as written; it cannot infer that a missing value was intended to mean zero.' },
        { title: 'Install and verify Python', text: 'Install a current Python 3 release from python.org. On Windows, enable the launcher during installation and use py where these examples use python3. Open a terminal, check the version, create a folder called reporting-lab, and open that folder in a text editor. Save scripts with a .py extension, not .py.txt. The interactive prompt is useful for experiments; a saved script is easier to repeat and share.', code: 'python3 --version\nmkdir reporting-lab\ncd reporting-lab\n# Save the next example as report.py, then run:\npython3 report.py', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Calculate in cents, format at the boundary', text: 'Keep money as integer cents in this first example so that binary floating-point rounding does not change your totals. A variable gives a value a meaningful name. The plus operator adds numbers, while the f-string inserts a calculated value into a message. The format specification .2f displays two decimal places. Formatting changes the presentation; it is not a substitute for correct arithmetic.', code: 'invoice_a = 1250\ninvoice_b = 800\ninvoice_c = 495\n\ntotal_cents = invoice_a + invoice_b + invoice_c\nprint(f"Weekly total: EUR {total_cents / 100:.2f}")\n# Weekly total: EUR 25.45', language: 'python' },
        { title: 'Read the error before changing the code', text: 'If you see NameError, compare the variable spelling at the reported line with its definition. If Python cannot open report.py, check your current directory and the file extension. Change one thing at a time and rerun the script. A successful program should produce a result you can independently check, not merely finish without an error.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Add a fourth invoice', instructions: ['Run the example and confirm EUR 25.45.', 'Add an invoice worth 305 cents without changing the original three.', 'Print both the number of invoices and the new total.', 'Change one variable name intentionally, observe the error, and fix it.'], solution: 'Add invoice_d = 305 and include it in total_cents. The new total is 2850 cents, or EUR 28.50, for four invoices. A misspelled variable should produce NameError; defining a different variable does not automatically rename its earlier uses.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Why does the example store invoice amounts as integer cents?', options: ['To avoid rounding problems in the additions', 'Because Python cannot represent decimal numbers', 'Because print only accepts integers'], answer: 0, explanation: 'Python supports floating-point numbers, but some decimal fractions cannot be represented exactly. Integer cents make these additions exact.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Represent records using dictionaries and lists.', 'Select rows with a condition.', 'Calculate a total using a loop and verify the result.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Move from individual variables to records', text: 'A dictionary groups named fields about one item. A list holds a sequence of items. Together they let you model a spreadsheet: each dictionary is a row, and each key is a column name. Consistent field names matter. Accessing row["amount_cents"] fails if the field does not exist, which is useful when a supposedly complete record is malformed.' },
        { title: 'Filter before you add', text: 'Revenue depends on a definition. Here, only paid orders count. A pending order has not been paid, and a refunded order is excluded from this simplified metric. The for loop visits each record; the if statement decides whether it contributes. Python uses indentation to group statements, so both the total and counter updates belong inside the condition.', code: 'orders = [\n    {"id": "A1", "status": "paid", "amount_cents": 2500},\n    {"id": "A2", "status": "pending", "amount_cents": 1800},\n    {"id": "A3", "status": "paid", "amount_cents": 900},\n    {"id": "A4", "status": "refunded", "amount_cents": 700},\n]\n\ntotal_cents = 0\npaid_count = 0\nfor order in orders:\n    if order["status"] == "paid":\n        total_cents += order["amount_cents"]\n        paid_count += 1\n\nprint(f"{paid_count} paid orders: EUR {total_cents / 100:.2f}")\n# 2 paid orders: EUR 34.00', language: 'python' },
        { title: 'Assignment is different from comparison', text: 'A single equals sign stores a value in a variable. Two equals signs compare values and produce True or False. Strings are case-sensitive: "Paid" is different from "paid". Decide whether to reject or normalize inconsistent input rather than silently losing records. The next lessons introduce functions and validation to make that decision explicit.' },
        { title: 'Check boundary cases', text: 'A good first test is an empty list: the total and count should both be zero. Next try a list containing only pending orders. Then add another paid order and calculate the expected answer by hand. These checks expose mistakes such as adding every order before checking its status or resetting the total inside the loop.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Summarize paid orders', instructions: ['Add a paid order for 600 cents and verify the new result.', 'Print the IDs of paid orders only.', 'Run the program with an empty list.', 'Explain what your metric does with refunded orders.'], solution: 'The updated result is 3 paid orders: EUR 40.00. Print order["id"] inside the if block to show A1, A3, and your new ID. An empty list produces 0 paid orders: EUR 0.00. Refunded orders are excluded; this is not a complete accounting model for partial refunds or taxes.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What happens if total_cents = 0 is moved inside the loop?', options: ['Python automatically calculates a subtotal', 'The accumulated total is reset on every iteration', 'Only unpaid orders are counted'], answer: 1, explanation: 'An accumulator must be initialized before the loop. Resetting it inside the loop discards earlier results.' },
    },
  ],
  'sql-data-analysis': [
    {
      objectives: ['Create a small relational dataset.', 'Explain primary and foreign keys.', 'Select specific columns from a table.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Think in rows, columns, and relationships', text: 'A relational database stores structured tables and enforces rules about their relationships. In this store, one customer can have many orders. Each customers row represents one person; each orders row represents one order. This unit of meaning is called the row grain. A primary key identifies a row uniquely. A foreign key ties an order to a customer that exists.' },
        { title: 'Create an isolated practice database', text: 'Use a local PostgreSQL installation or a disposable training database. In psql or pgAdmin, create a database named edunode_lab and connect to it before running the SQL below. Run this setup once in an empty database. The examples use integer cents for money and DATE for calendar dates. Later lessons reuse these exact tables, so keep the setup script.', code: "CREATE TABLE customers (\n  id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n  name text NOT NULL,\n  country text\n);\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n  id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n  customer_id integer REFERENCES customers(id),\n  ordered_on date NOT NULL,\n  status text NOT NULL,\n  amount_cents integer NOT NULL CHECK (amount_cents >= 0)\n);\nINSERT INTO customers VALUES\n  (1, 'Ada', 'AT'), (2, 'Lin', 'DE'), (3, 'Sam', NULL);\nINSERT INTO orders VALUES\n  (101, 1, '2026-01-10', 'paid', 2500),\n  (102, 1, '2026-02-05', 'paid', 1500),\n  (103, 2, '2026-02-07', 'pending', 4000),\n  (104, 2, '2026-02-09', 'paid', 3000),\n  (105, 2, '2026-02-10', 'refunded', 1000);", language: 'sql' },
        { title: 'Ask for the columns you need', text: 'SELECT names the output columns and FROM chooses the source table. A semicolon ends the statement. Use explicit column names in a report so a future table change does not unexpectedly change its output. SELECT * is convenient for a quick inspection, but a database does not guarantee row order unless you ask for one.', code: 'SELECT id, status, amount_cents\nFROM orders\nORDER BY id;\n\nSELECT id, name, country\nFROM customers\nORDER BY id;', language: 'sql' },
        { title: 'Check that the setup worked', text: 'You should see five orders and three customers. Customer 1 has two orders, customer 2 has three, and customer 3 has none. This last case is intentional: it will reveal the difference between an inner join and a left join later. NULL in country means the value is unknown, not that the country is an empty string.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Explore the store', instructions: ['Create the two tables and load the sample rows.', 'Return only name and country from customers.', 'Return only id and ordered_on from orders, ordered by id.', 'Identify the customer with no orders by inspecting the records.'], solution: 'Use SELECT name, country FROM customers ORDER BY id; and SELECT id, ordered_on FROM orders ORDER BY id;. Sam, customer 3, has no matching customer_id in orders. The setup intentionally includes this unmatched customer for later join exercises.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What does one row in the orders table represent?', options: ['One customer', 'One product category', 'One order'], answer: 2, explanation: 'The table grain is one order. Several orders can refer to the same customer, so counting orders is different from counting customers.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Combine filters with AND and OR.', 'Return a deterministic sorted result.', 'Handle unknown values using IS NULL.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Turn a question into a predicate', text: 'Start with a precise question: which paid orders are worth at least EUR 20? WHERE keeps only rows whose predicate evaluates to true. Amounts are stored in cents, so EUR 20 is 2000. Filtering a numeric column against a string or mixing euros and cents creates confusing results. Keep units visible in column names and examples.', code: "SELECT id, amount_cents\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'paid' AND amount_cents >= 2000\nORDER BY amount_cents DESC, id ASC;\n-- 104 | 3000\n-- 101 | 2500", language: 'sql' },
        { title: 'Use parentheses to show your intent', text: 'AND binds more tightly than OR. If you want paid orders from either January or February, group the date conditions explicitly or use a range. A half-open range includes the starting boundary and excludes the ending boundary. It also transfers well to timestamp columns, where a last-day-at-midnight cutoff could exclude most of that day.', code: "SELECT id, ordered_on, amount_cents\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'paid'\n  AND ordered_on >= DATE '2026-02-01'\n  AND ordered_on < DATE '2026-03-01'\nORDER BY ordered_on, id;\n-- Orders 102 and 104", language: 'sql' },
        { title: 'NULL is an unknown value', text: 'Comparing NULL with an equals sign does not return true, even when both sides are NULL. Use IS NULL or IS NOT NULL. A condition such as country <> \'AT\' will also omit unknown countries, because the comparison is unknown. If the business question needs those rows, include OR country IS NULL explicitly.', code: "SELECT name FROM customers WHERE country IS NULL;\n-- Sam\nSELECT name FROM customers\nWHERE country <> 'AT' OR country IS NULL\nORDER BY name;\n-- Lin, Sam", language: 'sql' },
        { title: 'LIMIT is not a ranking rule', text: 'LIMIT 2 returns at most two rows. To ask for the two largest orders, sort by amount first. Add a unique tie-breaker such as id so repeated executions produce a stable result when amounts match. This is especially important for pagination and for reproducible reports.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Find a focused set of orders', instructions: ['Return paid orders worth at least 1500 cents, largest first.', 'Return customers whose country is unknown.', 'Return the two newest orders, breaking ties by id.', 'Explain why country = NULL is incorrect.'], solution: 'The paid orders are 104 (3000), 101 (2500), and 102 (1500). Sam has an unknown country. ORDER BY ordered_on DESC, id DESC LIMIT 2 returns 105 and 104. Equality with NULL produces an unknown result, so use IS NULL to test for missing values.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Which condition finds customers with a missing country?', options: ['country IS NULL', 'country = NULL', "country = 'NULL'"], answer: 0, explanation: 'IS NULL tests for the absence of a value. The string NULL is ordinary text and is different from the SQL null marker.' },
    },
  ],
  'ai-workflow-automation': [
    {
      objectives: ['Describe a workflow as trigger, transformation, and action.', 'Identify where fixed rules are sufficient.', 'Define a measurable acceptance check before using AI.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Choose a task with a clear boundary', text: 'Consider a small support inbox. A new request arrives, someone decides whether it is billing or technical, and a reply is drafted. Automating everything at once makes failures difficult to explain. Start with one outcome: produce a review record containing the request ID, a category, a draft, and a reason for escalation. Sending a message is a separate action that needs explicit approval.' },
        { title: 'Draw the path before choosing tools', text: 'The trigger starts an execution. A transformation changes or enriches data. A decision branches the path. An action changes another system. List the fields that each step requires and produces. Mark where information could be missing or untrusted. In n8n, these steps become nodes connected in execution order.', code: 'Manual sample request\n  -> validate required fields\n  -> classify billing / technical / other\n  -> create a draft from an approved policy\n  -> store a review record\n  -> stop and wait for a person', language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Use rules for exact requirements', text: 'A missing request ID, an empty email address, or a known priority flag can be checked with ordinary conditions. AI is useful for ambiguous language, such as distinguishing a billing question from a technical problem. It is not a reliable substitute for validating required fields. A model can return plausible but wrong text, so treat its response as input that must be checked.' },
        { title: 'Define success and failure', text: 'Create five fictional requests with expected categories. For a first prototype, success means that every request produces one review record, required fields are preserved, and no email is sent. Include an empty message and a message containing instructions to ignore the policy. Record what should happen in each case before running the workflow. These examples become a regression set when you change prompts later.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Write a workflow brief', instructions: ['Choose a repetitive task and name its trigger.', 'List its input fields and desired output.', 'Identify one rule-based decision and one possible AI decision.', 'Write three success checks and two failure cases.'], solution: 'For support triage: trigger = a new request; inputs = request_id, email, message; output = a review record. Validate an empty message with a rule and classify natural-language intent with AI. Check that IDs survive, categories come from an allowed list, and nothing is sent automatically. Missing IDs and invalid model output should route to manual review.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Which step should normally use a fixed rule rather than AI?', options: ['Interpreting a vague complaint', 'Checking whether request_id is missing', 'Drafting a friendly explanation'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Required-field validation has an exact answer. A deterministic condition is easier to test and more reliable for this task.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Run a manual n8n execution.', 'Transform data with Edit Fields.', 'Branch on a deterministic condition and inspect both paths.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Use sample data before connecting accounts', text: 'Open an n8n instance and create a new workflow. A cloud account may require a trial or paid plan; self-hosting is another option described in n8n documentation. You do not need a model API key for this lesson. Add a Manual Trigger so nothing runs on a schedule or responds to public traffic while you are learning.' },
        { title: 'Create a request with Edit Fields', text: 'Connect an Edit Fields (Set) node. Add the following fields in JSON mode, or add them individually in manual mapping. Execute the node and inspect its output. n8n passes items between nodes; each item contains a JSON object. Keep the request ID attached throughout the workflow so later results can be traced back to the original request.', code: '{\n  "request_id": "demo-001",\n  "email": "learner@example.com",\n  "message": "Please explain the charge on my invoice.",\n  "category": "billing",\n  "priority": "normal"\n}', language: 'json' },
        { title: 'Route the request with an IF node', text: 'Add an IF node after Edit Fields. Set the first value to the expression {{$json.category}}, choose a string equals comparison, and set the second value to billing. Connect the true output to an Edit Fields node that adds queue = billing-review. Connect false to another that adds queue = general-review. In both nodes, enable the option to include other input fields so request_id and message are preserved.' },
        { title: 'Inspect the execution, then change one input', text: 'Execute the entire workflow. The billing path should receive one item and the other path none. Change category to technical and rerun; the general path should receive the item. An execution showing green nodes is not enough: inspect the output values and confirm that the record still contains the original request ID. Node labels can vary slightly between n8n versions, but the data flow remains the same.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Test both branches', instructions: ['Build the manual workflow and run a billing request.', 'Run a technical request and confirm the false branch.', 'Remove category and observe which branch receives the item.', 'Save the workflow with a descriptive name.'], solution: 'Billing should produce queue = billing-review; technical and a missing category should take the general-review path. A missing field does not become a valid billing label. In a production workflow, add a validation branch before classification so incomplete input is explicitly marked for review.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Why keep request_id after every transformation?', options: ['It makes AI responses automatically correct', 'n8n requires that exact field name', 'It lets you trace outputs and detect duplicate processing'], answer: 2, explanation: 'A stable identifier connects each result to its input and supports later duplicate checks. It does not guarantee the content is correct.' },
    },
  ],
  'cybersecurity-fundamentals': [
    {
      objectives: ['Distinguish an asset, threat, vulnerability, and control.', 'Balance prevention, detection, and recovery.', 'Prioritize one realistic security improvement.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Protect something specific', text: 'Security begins with assets: the information, services, and accounts you need to protect. For a small online store, assets include customer addresses, administrator accounts, payment configuration, and the ability to accept orders. Confidentiality protects information from unauthorized disclosure. Integrity protects it from unauthorized changes. Availability means legitimate users can access the service when needed.' },
        { title: 'Separate the threat from the weakness', text: 'A threat is a possible harmful event, such as an attacker taking over an administrator account. A vulnerability is a weakness that makes the event easier, such as a reused password or an unpatched service. A control reduces the likelihood or impact. Use concrete sentences: "A stolen administrator password could allow someone to change payout details." This is more useful than a broad statement that hackers are dangerous.' },
        { title: 'Layer controls around an event', text: 'For account takeover, a unique password and phishing-resistant multifactor authentication help prevent access. Alerts for new sessions and changes to sensitive settings help detect abuse. A tested process to revoke sessions, restore configuration, and contact affected parties supports recovery. A backup is not a preventive control against stolen credentials; it solves a different part of the problem.', code: 'Asset: administrator account\nThreat: unauthorized changes to payout settings\nWeakness: reused password and no MFA\nPrevent: unique password, passkey, limited admin accounts\nDetect: login and configuration-change alerts\nRecover: revoke sessions, restore settings, review activity', language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Prioritize with evidence', text: 'Ask what is exposed, how plausible the event is, and how damaging it would be. A rarely used public admin account with a reused password may deserve attention before an internal cosmetic warning. Assign an owner and a verification step to each improvement. Practice only with supplied records or systems you own; authorization is part of defining the scope of a security exercise.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Build a three-row threat model', instructions: ['Choose a fictional small business.', 'List three important assets.', 'For each, describe one threat and one weakness.', 'Add preventive, detective, and recovery controls.', 'Choose the first improvement and explain your priority.'], solution: 'For a customer database: theft through a compromised account is a threat; overly broad permissions are a weakness. Restrict permissions, monitor unusual exports, and prepare credential rotation and incident procedures. For availability, separately consider tested backups and service recovery. Your priority should connect exposure and business impact to a concrete control.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Which item is a detective control?', options: ['An alert for an unusual administrator login', 'A unique password', 'Restoring data from a backup'], answer: 0, explanation: 'An alert helps identify a possible event. A password is preventive, while restoring a backup is a recovery action.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Trace a web request through DNS, transport, and HTTP.', 'Explain the purpose of a port.', 'Recognize the limits of HTTPS.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Follow a request from name to service', text: 'When you open an HTTPS URL, the browser identifies the hostname, resolves it to an address using DNS, establishes a connection, negotiates encryption, and sends an HTTP request. Modern browsers can reuse connections and cached DNS results. Some use HTTP/3 over QUIC rather than TCP; the central ideas remain naming, addressing, transport, and an application protocol.' },
        { title: 'Addresses identify hosts; ports identify services', text: 'An IP address points to a network interface. A port distinguishes services on that address. HTTPS commonly uses port 443 and HTTP port 80, but services can listen elsewhere. A firewall may permit or deny traffic based on addresses, ports, connection state, and other rules. A connection failure can result from DNS, routing, filtering, or an application that is not listening; it does not automatically prove an attack.' },
        { title: 'Observe a request in your browser', text: 'Open the developer tools Network panel, then visit https://example.com. Select the document request and inspect its URL, status, response headers, and timing. A 200 status normally indicates success; a redirect uses a 3xx code. The Security panel or browser connection information can show certificate details. Interfaces vary, so focus on identifying the hostname and the protected connection rather than a particular icon.' },
        { title: 'Understand what TLS guarantees', text: 'TLS protects data in transit and allows the browser to verify a certificate for the hostname it contacted. It does not promise that the business operating that hostname is honest, that a downloaded file is safe, or that an application has no vulnerabilities. A phishing site can use valid HTTPS. Verify the actual hostname and the context of the request.', code: 'https://accounts.example.com/settings\n        └─ hostname: accounts.example.com\n\nhttps://example.com.attacker.test/settings\n        └─ hostname: example.com.attacker.test\n\nThe second hostname is not a subdomain of example.com.', language: 'text' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Explain a browser request', instructions: ['Inspect a request to example.com in the Network panel.', 'Record the hostname, status, and protocol if shown.', 'Explain the difference between DNS lookup and HTTP response.', 'Explain why a valid certificate does not make every website trustworthy.'], solution: 'DNS resolves a name into an address; HTTP communicates with the application after a connection is established. TLS protects that communication and authenticates the contacted hostname through certificates. A site operator can still publish deceptive content, so transport security and trust in the operator are separate questions.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What does a valid HTTPS connection establish?', options: ['The website cannot contain phishing content', 'Every file on the website is safe', 'The connection is encrypted and the certificate matches the contacted hostname'], answer: 2, explanation: 'HTTPS protects the transport and verifies the hostname relationship. It does not certify the honesty or security of the entire application.' },
    },
  ],
  'docker-cloud-deployment': [
    {
      objectives: ['Navigate a project directory and run a process.', 'Describe an HTTP port and a health endpoint.', 'Start a local Python service and verify its response.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Understand the process before packaging it', text: 'A container runs a process with an isolated view of resources. Before creating one, understand how the process runs on your laptop. A working directory affects relative file paths. Environment variables provide configuration. A listening port allows clients to connect. In this course, you will build a small Flask API with a health endpoint and package it using Gunicorn as the application server.' },
        { title: 'Create the application', text: 'Create a folder named cloud-lab. Save this code as app.py. The /health route returns a small JSON response and does not expose configuration or credentials. Keep a health endpoint inexpensive and avoid requiring an external database for a simple liveness check. Later operational checks can distinguish whether the process is alive from whether all dependencies are ready.', code: 'from flask import Flask\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.get("/health")\ndef health():\n    return {"status": "ok"}, 200\n', language: 'python' },
        { title: 'Install dependencies in an isolated environment', text: 'A virtual environment separates project packages from the system Python. The activation command below is for macOS and Linux. On Windows PowerShell use .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1; Gunicorn runs on Unix, so use WSL for the Gunicorn exercises or Flask locally until the Docker lesson. After installation, save the resolved dependency versions for reproducibility.', code: 'python3 -m venv .venv\n. .venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install "Flask>=3.1,<4" "gunicorn>=23,<24"\npython -m pip freeze > requirements.txt\ngunicorn --bind 127.0.0.1:8080 app:app\n# In a second terminal:\ncurl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/health', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Check the boundary of the service', text: 'The bind address 127.0.0.1 accepts connections from your machine only. Port 8080 is the chosen service endpoint; another process cannot normally bind the same address and port at the same time. Expect HTTP 200 and a JSON object containing status = ok. Stop the process with Ctrl+C and confirm that the request now fails. This proves you are testing the intended process.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Run and inspect the API', instructions: ['Create app.py and requirements.txt.', 'Start the service and request /health.', 'Request /missing and observe the 404 response.', 'Stop the service, then repeat the health request.'], solution: 'The running /health endpoint returns 200 and {"status":"ok"}. /missing returns 404 because no route matches. After shutdown, curl should report a connection failure, not an HTTP 500; the application is no longer available to produce an HTTP response.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Why bind a local practice service to 127.0.0.1?', options: ['It restricts incoming connections to the local machine', 'It encrypts all HTTP traffic', 'It automatically enables cloud deployment'], answer: 0, explanation: 'Loopback limits the listening interface. It does not provide encryption or deploy the service elsewhere.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Distinguish an image from a running container.', 'Build a Docker image from a Dockerfile.', 'Map a local port and inspect container logs.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Package the application and its runtime', text: 'Install Docker Engine or Docker Desktop and confirm that docker version can contact the daemon. An image is a packaged filesystem and runtime configuration. A container is an instance of that image with a running process and a writable layer. Rebuilding an image does not update an already running container; you must start a new container from the new image.' },
        { title: 'Write the first Dockerfile', text: 'Place this Dockerfile beside app.py and requirements.txt from lesson 1. COPY only the files the application needs. Install dependencies before copying the app so Docker can reuse the dependency layer when only application code changes. Create an unprivileged user. Inside a container, bind to 0.0.0.0 so traffic arriving through its network interface reaches Gunicorn.', code: 'FROM python:3.12-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\nRUN useradd --create-home appuser\nCOPY app.py .\nUSER appuser\nEXPOSE 8080\nCMD ["gunicorn", "--bind", "0.0.0.0:8080", "app:app"]', language: 'dockerfile' },
        { title: 'Build, run, and verify', text: 'The final dot in docker build is the build context. The tag names the image locally. The -p option publishes host port 8080 to container port 8080; binding the host side to 127.0.0.1 keeps this practice service local. EXPOSE documents the intended port but does not publish it by itself.', code: 'docker build -t edunode-health:local .\ndocker run -d --name edunode-health \\\n  -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 edunode-health:local\ncurl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/health\ndocker logs edunode-health\ndocker stop edunode-health\ndocker rm edunode-health', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Diagnose the common failures', text: 'If port 8080 is already occupied, stop the local process from lesson 1 or publish host port 8081 instead. If the container exits, inspect docker logs before rebuilding. If the app works inside the container but is unreachable outside, check both the bind address and the port mapping. Removing a stopped container does not remove the image.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Change the host port', instructions: ['Build and run the sample image.', 'Confirm the JSON response and inspect the logs.', 'Recreate the container with host port 8081 mapped to container port 8080.', 'Request http://127.0.0.1:8081/health and clean up the container.'], solution: 'Use -p 127.0.0.1:8081:8080 on the new docker run command. The application still listens on 8080 inside the container, while your browser uses host port 8081. Stop and remove the named container when finished; the reusable image remains on your machine.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What does EXPOSE 8080 do in a Dockerfile?', options: ['Publishes port 8080 on every network interface', 'Documents the intended container port without publishing it', 'Starts an HTTP server'], answer: 1, explanation: 'EXPOSE is metadata. Publishing requires a runtime option such as -p, and a process must actually listen on the port.' },
    },
  ],
  'javascript-web-fundamentals': [
    {
      objectives: ['Create an HTML file and open it in a browser.', 'Use headings, paragraphs, lists, and links.', 'Describe elements, tags, and attributes.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'A web page is a text file with structure', text: 'A browser reads an HTML document and builds a tree of elements called the DOM. You write the structure; the browser renders it. HTML describes what things are — a heading, a paragraph, a link — while CSS (next lesson) controls how they look. Because the source is plain text, you can open any web page in a browser and choose "view source" to see exactly what the author wrote.' },
        { title: 'Create your first file', text: 'Create a folder called web-lab and a file named index.html inside it. Save the document below exactly as shown, then double-click the file to open it in your browser. You should see the heading and paragraph rendered. The .html extension matters: it tells the operating system and the browser to treat the file as a web page.', code: '<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang="en">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset="utf-8">\n    <title>My learning page</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Hello, web</h1>\n    <p>I am learning how pages are built.</p>\n  </body>\n</html>', language: 'html' },
        { title: 'Elements, tags, and attributes', text: 'An element usually has an opening tag, content, and a closing tag: <p>text</p>. Some elements are self-contained, like <img> or <meta>. Attributes add information inside the opening tag — href tells a link where to go, lang declares the document language. The head section holds metadata that is not displayed on the page; the title inside it appears on the browser tab, not in the page body.', code: '<h2>Study resources</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org">MDN Web Docs</a></li>\n  <li><a href="https://web.dev/learn">web.dev Learn</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tip: a list item nests inside a list element.</p>', language: 'html' },
        { title: 'Make changes and refresh', text: 'Web development is a tight edit-refresh loop: change the file, save, reload the browser tab. Try renaming the h1 and watch the page change. Try deleting a closing tag and see how the browser guesses at your intent — browsers are forgiving, which is convenient but can hide structural mistakes. Keep a validator in mind for later, and learn to spot when a missing </li> or <p> makes the rest of the page render oddly.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Build a one-page learner profile', instructions: ['Create index.html with a proper doctype, head, and body.', 'Add your name as an h1 and a short bio as a paragraph.', 'Add an unordered list of three things you want to learn.', 'Link one list item to a real documentation site.'], solution: 'Your page should show your name as the largest heading, one bio paragraph, and a bulleted list with at least one clickable link. A missing closing tag usually still renders, but a missing <body> or misplaced </html> can confuse later edits, so keep the structure from the example.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Where does the content inside <title> appear?', options: ['As the largest heading on the page', 'On the browser tab and in search results', 'In the address bar'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The title element sets the tab label and is often used as the link text in search results; it does not render in the page body.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Link a CSS file to an HTML page.', 'Use selectors for elements, classes, and IDs.', 'Arrange elements with the box model and flexbox.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'CSS assigns rules to elements', text: 'CSS is a set of rules. Each rule has a selector that picks elements and a declaration block with property: value pairs. The cascade decides which rule wins when several apply: later rules, more specific selectors, and inline styles override earlier general ones. Classes (.name) are the workhorse of everyday styling; IDs (#name) are unique per page and best reserved for anchors or scripting.' },
        { title: 'Link an external stylesheet', text: 'Create style.css next to index.html and link it in the head. Keeping CSS in its own file means one change updates every page that links it. After saving both files, reload: the background should turn dark and the heading color should change. If nothing happens, check the file name, the link path, and whether both files are in the same folder.', code: '<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">\n\n/* style.css */\nbody {\n  background: #f4f6f8;\n  color: #1c2733;\n  font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 24px;\n}\nh1 { color: #0a6b5b; }\n.card { background: #ffffff; padding: 16px; border-radius: 8px; }', language: 'css' },
        { title: 'Understand the box model', text: 'Every element is a rectangular box: content in the middle, surrounded by padding, then border, then margin. Padding is space inside the element; margin is space between elements. A common beginner surprise is that width sets the content width by default, so padding makes boxes bigger than expected — box-sizing: border-box includes padding in the width and is widely enabled.', code: '.card {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n  width: 100%;\n  padding: 16px;\n  margin-bottom: 12px;\n  border: 1px solid #d8dee6;\n}', language: 'css' },
        { title: 'Arrange items with flexbox', text: 'Flexbox lays out a row or column of items. Apply display: flex to a container, then control direction, spacing, and alignment. justify-content distributes items along the main axis; gap puts consistent space between them; flex-wrap lets items wrap to a new line on small screens. It is the right tool for nav bars, card rows, and centering.', code: '.resources {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 12px;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n}\n.resources .card {\n  flex: 1 1 180px;\n  margin-bottom: 0;\n}', language: 'css' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Style your learner profile', instructions: ['Create style.css and link it to your page.', 'Give the body a readable font, a light background, and page padding.', 'Wrap each list item in a .card and lay them out with flexbox.', 'Give one card a second class like .featured with a distinct accent color.'], solution: 'The stylesheet loads if colors and spacing visibly change. The cards should sit side by side on a wide screen and wrap on a narrow one. The featured card needs a rule like .featured { border-color: #0a6b5b; } — the class selector works on any element carrying that class.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Which selector targets <div class="note featured">?', options: ['#featured', '.featured', 'featured'], answer: 1, explanation: 'A leading dot selects a class. A leading # selects an ID, and a bare word selects an element type.' },
    },
  ],
  'git-github-collaboration': [
    {
      objectives: ['Explain what a Git repository records.', 'Initialize a repository and make the first commit.', 'Read git status and understand untracked versus tracked files.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'A history you can trust', text: 'Version control records snapshots of your project over time. Each snapshot is a commit: it captures the state of tracked files, an author, a timestamp, and a message explaining the change. With a history you can compare versions, find when a bug appeared, and recover work you thought was lost. Git stores this history locally in a .git directory — you do not need a server or an account to benefit from it.' },
        { title: 'Initialize a repository', text: 'Create a project folder, open a terminal inside it, and run git init. This creates the hidden .git directory that makes the folder a repository. Before your first commit, check that Git knows who you are: git config user.name and user.email identify the author recorded in each commit. These are usually set globally once per machine.', code: 'mkdir notes-app && cd notes-app\ngit init\ngit config --global user.name "Your Name"\ngit config --global user.email "you@example.com"\ngit status', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Stage and commit', text: 'Git tracks changes through a staging area: you add the files you want in the next snapshot, then commit them. Create a file called notes.txt, write a line of text, and run the sequence below. git status moves the file from untracked to staged to committed. This two-step flow lets you commit only part of your work when files are unrelated — a habit that keeps history readable.', code: 'echo "# My notes" > notes.txt\ngit status          # untracked file\ngit add notes.txt\ngit status          # staged for commit\ngit commit -m "Add notes file with project title"\ngit status          # clean working tree', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Small commits, clear messages', text: 'A good commit does one thing and its message completes the sentence "This commit will…". Commit early and often: small snapshots are easier to review, revert, and understand than one giant change. A commit is local — nothing leaves your machine until you push to a remote, which a later lesson covers.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Create a history of two commits', instructions: ['Initialize a repository in a new folder.', 'Create a file and commit it with a clear message.', 'Edit the file and commit the change separately.', 'Run git status after each step and describe what changed.'], solution: 'After the first commit the file is tracked; the second edit shows as modified until staged and committed again. Two commits means two snapshots, each with its own author, timestamp, and message. git status should report a clean working tree when finished.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What does git add notes.txt do?', options: ['Immediately commits the file', 'Stages the file for the next commit', 'Uploads the file to GitHub'], answer: 1, explanation: 'git add moves changes into the staging area. Nothing is recorded in history until git commit, and nothing is shared until you push to a remote.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Write commit messages that explain intent.', 'Inspect changes with git diff and history with git log.', 'Stage only part of your work when appropriate.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Read the state of your work', text: 'git status tells you what changed but not what the change contains. git diff shows unstaged edits line by line — removed lines prefixed with -, added lines with +. git diff --staged (or --cached) shows exactly what the next commit will contain. Reviewing the staged diff before committing catches accidental edits, debug code, and files you did not mean to include.', code: 'git diff            # working tree vs last staged/committed state\ngit diff --staged   # exactly what the next commit will record\ngit log --oneline   # compact history of commits\ngit log -p -1       # full patch of the most recent commit', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Stage selectively', text: 'When one file contains two unrelated changes, you can still keep commits focused: stage the file you want and leave the rest, or use git add -p to review hunks and stage them individually. Selective staging takes a moment longer but means a future git revert undoes one logical change instead of an unrelated bundle.', code: 'git add index.html\ngit commit -m "Add footer with contact link"\n# script.js still has unstaged work — it stays out of this commit', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Write useful messages', text: 'The first line is a summary — under ~50 characters, imperative mood: "Fix date formatting in report" not "fixed stuff" or "update". If the change needs context, leave a blank line and explain why in the body. Your future self and your teammates read these messages when something breaks; "wip" and "asdf" tell them nothing.' },
        { title: 'Understand that history is append-only', text: 'Commits do not modify earlier snapshots; they add new ones that point to their parent. Deleting a file and committing does not remove it from history — it is still recoverable from an earlier commit. This is why accidentally committed secrets are a serious problem (covered later) and why history feels safe: almost nothing is truly lost.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Practice the review-then-commit habit', instructions: ['Make two unrelated edits in your notes file.', 'Review the unstaged diff before staging.', 'Stage only one file and commit it with a clear message.', 'Use git log --oneline and git log -p -1 to confirm the history.'], solution: 'git diff shows your edits before staging; git diff --staged shows exactly what you committed. The log lists two commits, newest first, and the patch view shows the added line(s). The uncommitted file still appears in git status.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Which command shows exactly what the next commit will contain?', options: ['git status', 'git diff --staged', 'git log -p'], answer: 1, explanation: 'git diff --staged compares the staging area against the last commit — the precise content that will be recorded next.' },
    },
  ],
  'networking-linux-fundamentals': [
    {
      objectives: ['Open a Linux terminal and run basic commands.', 'Navigate directories with pwd, ls, and cd.', 'Get help from man pages and command flags.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Why the command line matters', text: 'Servers, routers, containers, and cloud VMs usually have no graphical interface — the terminal is how real infrastructure is managed. A shell accepts text commands and returns text output. It is precise, scriptable, and identical whether you are on your laptop or a server on the other side of the world. Everything in this course assumes a Linux environment: a VM, WSL on Windows, or a free cloud shell.' },
        { title: 'Where am I? Navigation basics', text: 'The filesystem is a tree starting at /. Your location is the working directory. pwd prints it; ls lists contents; cd changes directory. ~ is your home directory, .. is the parent, and / alone is the root. Paths can be absolute (start at /) or relative to where you are. Tab completion fills in names — press Tab twice to see options.', code: 'pwd                 # print working directory\nls                  # list files\nls -la              # all files, detailed view, including hidden\ncd /etc             # move to /etc\ncd ..               # back up one level\ncd ~                # go home\nmkdir practice && cd practice', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Get help without memorizing', text: 'Every system tool documents itself. man ls opens the manual page (q quits). Most commands also accept --help for a shorter summary, and -h is a common shorter variant. Try ls --help and compare it with man ls. Learning to read help output is a career skill: options like -l (long format) and -a (all, including dotfiles) appear in nearly every tutorial you will follow.', code: 'man ls\nls --help | head -20\nhistory | tail -10   # review what you just ran', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Commands are just programs', text: 'When you type ls, the shell finds a program on your PATH and runs it. Arguments modify its behavior. Errors are normal and informative: "No such file or directory" usually means a typo or a wrong directory, not a broken system. Read the error, check where you are with pwd, and list what is actually there before assuming the file is gone.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Tour the filesystem', instructions: ['Print your working directory and list your home folder.', 'Create a practice directory, enter it, and confirm with pwd.', 'Read the ls manual to find the flag for human-readable sizes.', 'Run ls -lh in /var/log and identify the largest file shown.'], solution: 'pwd shows your location; ls -lh -S sorts by size with readable units, so the largest file is listed first. The practice directory appears under your home path. If a command reports "command not found", check the spelling — the tool may not be installed on your distribution.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What does pwd print?', options: ['Your password in plain text', 'The current working directory', 'A list of files'], answer: 1, explanation: 'pwd = print working directory — your current location in the filesystem tree.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Read file type and permission bits from ls -l.', 'Explain owner, group, and other permissions.', 'Change permissions safely with chmod.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Every file has an owner and permission bits', text: 'Run ls -l and each line starts with ten characters: the first is the type (- for file, d for directory), followed by three triplets of rwx for owner, group, and others. r means read, w write, x execute. On a directory, x means you can enter it and r means you can list its contents. Permissions are why a normal user cannot accidentally edit system files — and why scripts sometimes refuse to run until made executable.', code: '-rw-r--r--  1 ana  staff   220 Mar  3 10:00 notes.txt\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 ana  staff    64 Mar  3 10:01 reports\n\n# notes.txt: owner ana can read+write, group and others can read\n# reports: a directory; everyone can enter and list it', language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Change permissions with chmod', text: 'chmod modifies permission bits. Symbolic form is readable: chmod u+x script.sh adds execute for the owner; chmod go-w file removes write for group and others. Numeric form encodes r=4, w=2, x=1 summed per triplet — 644 is rw-r--r--, 755 is rwxr-xr-x. Private files like SSH keys conventionally use 600: only you can read them.', code: 'chmod 644 notes.txt      # rw-r--r--\nchmod 755 run.sh         # rwxr-xr-x — executable\nchmod 600 secrets.txt    # rw------- owner only\nchmod u+x backup.sh      # add execute for owner only\nls -l', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Read and edit files safely', text: 'cat prints a file; less pages through it (space to page, / to search, q to quit) — prefer less for anything long. To create or append quickly, use redirection: echo "text" > file overwrites, >> appends. cp copies, mv moves or renames, rm deletes — and rm has no trash bin. Always check the filename twice before deleting, and prefer rm -i or a test with ls first on anything important.', code: 'cat notes.txt\nless /var/log/syslog    # on Debian/Ubuntu systems\necho "new line" >> notes.txt\ncp notes.txt notes-backup.txt\nls notes*               # verify before removing anything', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Why this matters beyond files', text: 'The same read/write/execute model underlies service security. Web servers run as a dedicated user precisely so a compromised process cannot overwrite system files — the principle of least privilege you met in the security course is enforced through these bits. When a deployment fails with "Permission denied", checking ls -l and who the process runs as is the correct first diagnostic, not blindly using chmod 777.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Create a private note and a runnable script', instructions: ['Create private.txt and set it so only you can read and write it.', 'Write a script containing echo "it works" and make it executable.', 'Run the script with ./filename and explain the leading ./.', 'Verify all permissions with ls -l.'], solution: 'chmod 600 private.txt and chmod 755 (or u+x) on the script. The ./ prefix tells the shell to run the file in the current directory — your working directory is not on PATH for security reasons. ls -l should show rw------- and rwxr-xr-x respectively.' },
      quiz: { question: 'A file shows -rw-r-----. What can members of its group do?', options: ['Read and write the file', 'Read the file only', 'Nothing'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The middle triplet r-- grants the owning group read access only; others get nothing.' },
    },
  ],
  'data-analysis-pandas': [
    {
      objectives: ['Install pandas and load a CSV into a DataFrame.', 'Inspect rows, columns, data types, and summary statistics.', 'Explain the difference between a DataFrame and a Series.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'A DataFrame is a labeled table', text: 'pandas is Python’s standard data-analysis library. A DataFrame is a table: labeled columns, an index of rows, and typed data in each column. A Series is a single labeled column. If the SQL course felt productive but you wanted loops, functions, and files, pandas is where those worlds meet — tables you can manipulate in code, in memory, without a database server.' },
        { title: 'Install and load data', text: 'Create a project folder, make a virtual environment, and install pandas. Then create sales.csv with the content below — a small but realistic export with an intentional duplicate and a missing value to clean later. read_csv parses the file into a DataFrame; it guesses types, which is usually right but worth checking.', code: 'python -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate\npip install "pandas>=2.2,<3"\n\n# sales.csv\norder_id,date,customer,region,amount\n1,2026-01-05,Ada,AT,120\n2,2026-01-07,Lin,DE,85\n3,2026-01-09,Ada,AT,40\n4,2026-01-12,Sam,,210\n5,2026-01-12,Sam,,210', language: 'shell' },
        { title: 'Inspect before analyzing', text: 'Never analyze data you have not inspected. head() shows the first rows; shape reports rows and columns; dtypes shows each column’s type; describe() summarizes numeric columns; info() lists non-null counts — the fastest way to spot missing data. df["amount"] selects one column and returns a Series; df[["amount", "region"]] returns a smaller DataFrame.', code: 'import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv("sales.csv")\nprint(df.head())\nprint(df.shape)          # (5, 5)\nprint(df.dtypes)         # amount is int64, region is object\nprint(df.describe())     # count, mean, min, max for numbers\ndf.info()                # region: 4 non-null — one is missing', language: 'python' },
        { title: 'Scripts and notebooks both work', text: 'Save the example as inspect.py and run it. Many analysts work in Jupyter notebooks, where each cell shows its output immediately — great for exploration. A plain .py script is better for repeatable pipelines. This course uses scripts so every step is reproducible; nothing here requires a notebook.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Profile the sales export', instructions: ['Create the CSV and load it with read_csv.', 'Report the shape, dtypes, and describe() output.', 'Find the column with missing values.', 'Select just the amount column and compute its mean with .mean().'], solution: 'Shape is (5, 5); region has one missing value (4 non-null of 5). df["amount"].mean() is 133.0. The duplicate row is invisible in head() but will matter when we clean in a later lesson — count(*) is not automatically unique records.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What does df.head(3) return?', options: ['The first three columns', 'The first three rows', 'Three random rows'], answer: 1, explanation: 'head(n) shows the first n rows of the DataFrame — columns all remain; use column selection to limit columns.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Select single and multiple columns.', 'Filter rows with boolean conditions.', 'Sort a DataFrame and chain operations readably.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Select columns deliberately', text: 'df["amount"] returns a Series; df[["amount", "region"]] returns a DataFrame with two columns — note the double brackets: the outer pair is indexing, the inner is a list of names. Selecting explicitly makes code readable and avoids accidentally relying on columns that later disappear when the export format changes.' },
        { title: 'Filter with boolean masks', text: 'A comparison like df["amount"] > 100 produces a Series of True/False values — a mask. Putting it inside brackets keeps only rows where the mask is True. Combine conditions with & (and) and | (or), each wrapped in parentheses because & binds more tightly than comparison operators. This is the same idea as WHERE in SQL, expressed as Python objects.', code: 'df[df["amount"] > 100]\ndf[df["region"] == "AT"]\ndf[(df["amount"] > 100) & (df["region"] == "DE")]\ndf[df["customer"].isin(["Ada", "Sam"])]', language: 'python' },
        { title: 'Sort and keep a deterministic order', text: 'sort_values orders rows by one or more columns, ascending by default. Add a tie-breaker column so repeated values produce a stable order every run — essential for tests and reproducible reports. The result is a new DataFrame; pandas rarely modifies in place unless you pass inplace=True or reassign.', code: 'df.sort_values("amount", ascending=False)\ndf.sort_values(["date", "order_id"])  # stable order\ntop3 = df.nlargest(3, "amount")', language: 'python' },
        { title: 'Chain steps readably', text: 'Real analysis reads as a pipeline: load, filter, compute. pandas supports method chaining, where each call returns a new DataFrame — write one transformation per line inside parentheses so reviewers can follow the logic. Assign intermediate results to named variables when a stage needs inspecting; clarity beats cleverness for a report someone will trust.', code: 'result = (\n    df[df["region"] == "AT"]\n    .sort_values("amount", ascending=False)\n    [["order_id", "customer", "amount"]]\n)\nprint(result)', language: 'python' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Find the orders that matter', instructions: ['List orders over 100 in descending amount order.', 'Show only orders from the AT region, sorted by date.', 'Select the three largest orders by amount.', 'Rewrite one query as a chain selecting only order_id, customer, and amount.'], solution: 'df[df["amount"] > 100].sort_values("amount", ascending=False) gives rows 4, 5 (210), then 1 (120). nlargest(3, "amount") returns the same top three. The chain from the last section produces the AT orders 1 and 3 in amount order: 120 then 40.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Why write (df["a"] > 1) & (df["b"] < 2) with parentheses?', options: ['Python requires them for comparisons', 'The & operator binds more tightly than comparisons, so they prevent wrong results', 'Pandas only accepts tuples'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Without parentheses, & evaluates before the comparisons and combines the wrong values — the classic boolean-mask bug.' },
    },
  ],
  'it-support-troubleshooting': [
    {
      objectives: ['Describe the scope of IT support roles.', 'Apply a six-step troubleshooting method.', 'Separate symptoms from causes.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Support is a structured craft', text: 'Help desk, service desk, desktop support, and field technician roles share one core skill: turning a vague report ("it is broken") into a working system and a documented fix. Good support is not knowing every answer — it is having a repeatable method that works on problems you have never seen, plus clear communication with the person affected.' },
        { title: 'The six-step method', text: 'Adapted from industry-standard frameworks: (1) Identify the problem — gather information and define what "broken" means. (2) Establish a theory of probable cause — list likely explanations starting with the simple ones. (3) Test the theory — confirm or eliminate it with a check. (4) Make a plan and implement. (5) Verify full functionality and take preventive steps. (6) Document findings, actions, and outcomes. Skipping to step 4 is how issues "fix themselves" and return tomorrow.', code: 'Report: "I can\'t print invoices."\n\n1. Identify: error dialog? all apps or one? since when?\n2. Theory: print spooler stopped > driver > network down\n3. Test: check spooler service, try test page, ping printer\n4. Plan: restart spooler, clear stuck job, retry\n5. Verify: user prints invoice AND a second document\n6. Document: cause, fix, prevention in the ticket', language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Symptoms are not causes', text: '"The computer is slow" is a symptom; a disk at 100% usage during a scheduled scan is a candidate cause. Fixing symptoms without finding the cause produces repeat tickets. Always ask what changed — updates, new hardware, new software, a power outage — because most failures correlate with a recent change. When a theory fails, the method says establish a new one or escalate, not guess randomly.' },
        { title: 'The person is part of the system', text: 'Users are experts in their workflow, not in your tooling. Translating "the thingy doesn\'t work" into a reproducible fault is itself a skill: ask what they were trying to do, what they expected, and what happened instead. Never make a user feel blamed — a ticket that says "user error" teaches nothing, while "expected file in wrong format, showed user the template" fixes the next occurrence too.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Map a report to the method', instructions: ['Take this report: "Wi-Fi drops every afternoon around 2pm."', 'Write one question you would ask for step 1.', 'List two candidate theories ordered by likelihood.', 'Write the test for your first theory and what result would confirm it.'], solution: 'Step 1: "Does it affect only you or the whole office, and does it recover on its own?" The daily timing suggests interference or congestion — theory: microwave/scheduled device interference, or DHCP lease/IP conflict. Test: check logs or run continuous ping at 1:55pm; packet loss starting at the same time confirms a timed interference source.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What is the first step of the troubleshooting method?', options: ['Reinstall the software', 'Identify the problem and gather information', 'Implement a fix and verify'], answer: 1, explanation: 'You cannot fix what you have not defined. Gathering information and identifying the actual problem comes before any theory or change.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Ask open versus closed questions deliberately.', 'Reproduce an issue or narrow its scope.', 'Identify what changed before a failure appeared.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Questions that extract facts', text: 'Closed questions ("Is the printer on?") get yes/no answers — fast for checking a theory. Open questions ("Walk me through what happens when you click Print") reveal steps, errors, and workarounds the user forgot to mention. Start open to map the territory, then close down on specifics. Repeat the user\'s answer back in your own words: "So it fails only in Excel, printing to the color printer, since Tuesday — did I get that right?"' },
        { title: 'Reproduce or bound the problem', text: 'If you can reproduce it, you can fix it — reproduction converts a story into an experiment. Ask the user to demonstrate, then repeat the same steps yourself. If it is intermittent, bound it: does it happen for all users? on other machines? on Wi-Fi versus cable? at a specific time? Each "does it also happen when…" halves the search space. One failing account on a working machine points at the profile; everyone failing points at shared infrastructure.', code: 'Scope questions that bisect the problem:\n- Only you, or your whole team?\n- Only this app, or all apps?\n- Only on Wi-Fi, or also on ethernet/phone hotspot?\n- Always, or only at certain times?\n- Before and after what change?', language: 'text' },
        { title: 'What changed?', text: 'Most outages correlate with a change: an update, a new device, a moved cable, a new account, a power cut. Ask "when did it last work?" and "what happened between then and now?" Check the obvious recent events first — the morning update window is a better suspect than an exotic driver issue. Document the change window in the ticket; it is often the detail the next technician needs.' },
        { title: 'Record facts, not interpretations', text: 'Write the observable fact: "Error 0x80070005 when clicking Save in Invoicer 3.2; affects 2 of 12 tested users; started after Thursday\'s update." Avoid "the system is broken" or "user can\'t save" — the first is unhelpful, the second blames. A ticket full of facts lets a colleague (or you, next month) resume the diagnosis without starting over.' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Interview for a vague report', instructions: ['Report: "The internet is down."', 'Write two open questions to start with.', 'Write three scoping questions that narrow the fault.', 'Record one factual sentence for the ticket summarizing what you learned.'], solution: 'Open: "What are you trying to do, and what exactly happens?" / "When did you first notice this?" Scope: "Is it all websites or one?", "Does your phone on the same Wi-Fi work?", "Are colleagues affected?" A fact: "Browser shows DNS error for all sites since ~09:10; colleague\'s machine on same VLAN works; mobile data works on the same laptop."'},
      quiz: { question: 'Why ask "When did it last work?"', options: ['To prove the user waited too long to report', 'To identify what changed between working and broken states', 'Because SLA timers start at that answer'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Failures usually correlate with a recent change. Bounding the working window narrows the suspect list to updates, moves, and new installs in that period.' },
    },
  ],
  'stablecoins-in-practice': [
    {
      objectives: ['Define a stablecoin and name the problem it solves.', 'Distinguish a stablecoin from volatile crypto and from a bank deposit.', 'Identify the three questions to ask before trusting a peg.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'The problem: you cannot price in volatility', text: "A currency that moves ten percent in a day is hard to invoice in, pay salaries in, or save in. Bitcoin and ETH are excellent at being scarce digital assets and poor at being stable units of account. Stablecoins exist to close that gap: they are blockchain tokens engineered to track a familiar unit of value, most often one US dollar. The goal is boring on purpose — a token that behaves like digital cash rather than a speculative asset." },
        { title: 'What the token actually is', text: "A stablecoin is a claim. For fiat-backed coins like USDC, it is a claim on dollars held in reserve by an issuer: Circle holds cash and short-term Treasuries, and each token is a transferable IOU redeemable for one dollar. On-chain, the token is just a balance entry — on Ethereum a smart contract balance, on Stellar an issued asset in your account. The stability is not in the token; it is in whatever stands behind it.", code: "Thinking checklist for any stablecoin:\n1. What backs it?        (reserves? collateral? nothing?)\n2. What can I redeem it for? (dollars? collateral? a promise?)\n3. Who can mint, freeze, or redeem?", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'A mental model: the casino chip', text: "A fiat-backed stablecoin behaves like a casino chip. You hand the cashier a dollar, you get a chip worth a dollar inside the casino, and you can trade it freely with other players. As long as everyone trusts the cashier will pay out, the chip trades at par. The moment that trust cracks — slow redemptions, opaque reserves, a bank failure — the chip trades at a discount. Later lessons show exactly how that discount forms and when it heals." },
        { title: 'Why it matters beyond trading', text: "Stablecoins settle remittances in seconds for fractions of a cent, give savers in high-inflation economies access to digital dollars, and serve as the base currency of DeFi. They are also how many businesses first touch blockchain: a supplier invoice paid in USDC is ordinary accounting, not a bet on token price. That practical role is why this course treats them as financial infrastructure rather than a trading instrument." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Evaluate three tokens', instructions: ['Pick USDC, USDT, and DAI.', 'For each, write one sentence answering: what backs it?', 'Write one sentence answering: what can it be redeemed for?', 'Note which answer you could not verify quickly.'], solution: 'USDC: backed by cash and short-term Treasuries; redeemable 1:1 with Circle. USDT: backed by Tether-reported reserves; redeemable with Tether for verified customers. DAI: backed by on-chain collateral vaults; redeemable for collateral through the protocol. If the redemption answer was hard to find, that difficulty is itself a risk signal worth recording.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Where does a fiat-backed stablecoin get its stability?', options: ['From the blockchain it runs on', 'From the reserves the issuer holds and the right to redeem at par', 'From the number of people who hold it'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The token is a transferable claim on issuer-held reserves. Confidence in redemption keeps the market price near one dollar; the network only moves the claim.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Explain how arbitrage and redemption hold a peg.', 'Describe a mint/redeem cycle for USDC.', 'Recognize the early signs that a peg is under stress.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Arbitrage is the engine', text: "Suppose USDC trades on an exchange at $0.98. A trader can buy ten thousand tokens for $9,800, redeem them with Circle for $10,000, and keep the $200. Their buying on the exchange pushes the price up; repeated until the gap closes. The same works in reverse: above $1.00, traders mint at par and sell into the premium. The peg is not enforced by decree — it is maintained by profit-seekers, and it only works because redemption at par is real.", code: "If price < $1: buy on market -> redeem with issuer at $1 -> profit\nIf price > $1: mint with issuer at $1 -> sell on market -> profit\nRepeat until the gap costs more than the fees to close it.", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'A mint and redeem cycle with USDC', text: "A business wires $50,000 to Circle; Circle mints 50,000 USDC to its address. The tokens move freely on-chain — payments, DeFi, remittances. When a holder wants dollars back, they send USDC to Circle, the tokens are burned, and dollars are wired out. Mint creates supply against deposits; burn destroys supply against withdrawals. On Stellar this same lifecycle happens through anchors — regulated on/off-ramps that issue and redeem assets using standard protocols." },
        { title: 'Why prices still wiggle', text: "Arbitrage takes time and costs money: wire transfers take hours, exchange withdrawals take minutes, and traders demand a spread for the service. So in fast markets you will see USDC print $0.998 or $1.002 — noise inside the arbitrage band. A healthy peg wiggles in a tight band and snaps back. Watch for drift that does not snap back, or a widening gap between venues: that is stress, not noise." },
        { title: 'Reading the warning signs', text: "Three signals deserve attention: a price persistently below $0.995 across venues, redemption delays or new withdrawal limits announced by the issuer, and unusual reserve or attestation news. USDC's brief move to $0.87 in March 2023 showed all three at once — reserves trapped at a failing bank — and also showed recovery once the backing was confirmed safe. A peg under stress is a claim on reserves repricing in real time." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Walk the arbitrage', instructions: ['Imagine USDC trades at $0.97 on exchange A.', 'Write out each step an arbitrageur takes and where profit appears.', 'List two costs that shrink the profit.', 'Explain in one sentence why the peg recovers.'], solution: 'Buy USDC on A for $0.97, redeem with Circle for $1.00, keep $0.03 minus wire, exchange, and trading fees. The buying pressure on A lifts the price toward $1; once the gap is smaller than total costs, arbitrage stops and the market is back inside the normal band.' },
      quiz: { question: 'USDC trades at $0.97. What does the arbitrageur do?', options: ['Sells USDC short and waits for it to fall further', 'Buys USDC cheap and redeems it with the issuer at par', 'Mints new USDC and sells it on the exchange'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Below peg, profit comes from buying discounted tokens and redeeming at par. Minting and selling is the profitable move only when the price is above one dollar.' },
    },
  ],
  'ethereum-development': [
    {
      objectives: ['Describe Ethereum as a shared state machine.', 'Distinguish externally owned accounts from contract accounts.', 'Explain why every state change costs gas.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'A world computer, in one paragraph', text: "Ethereum is a single logical computer run by thousands of independent machines. It has a state — every account balance and every contract's storage — and it applies transactions that move the state forward in agreed steps called blocks. Nobody owns the computer, everyone can check its work, and programs deployed to it (smart contracts) run exactly as written. That is the whole idea; everything else is mechanics." },
        { title: 'Two kinds of accounts', text: "An externally owned account (EOA) is controlled by a private key — that is you, holding ETH in a wallet. A contract account is controlled by code — it has a balance and storage but no private key; it acts only when a transaction or another contract calls it. Both have addresses, both can hold ETH and tokens, and both appear in the same global state. Most confusion in Ethereum resolves once you ask: is this address a key-holder or a program?", code: "EOA:        key pair -> signs transactions -> starts everything\nContract:   code + storage -> runs when called -> cannot start anything\n\nEvery transaction chain begins with an EOA signature.", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'State, not files', text: "Ethereum does not 'run apps' the way a server does; it transitions state. A contract's variables live in its storage slot of the global state tree. When you 'use' a dApp, you send a transaction that asks the network to apply a function and update storage. Every node re-executes the same function and must reach the same result — determinism is why blockchains can agree on outcomes without trusting each other." },
        { title: 'Why gas exists', text: "Unlimited free computation on a public machine is a denial-of-service invitation: anyone could submit an infinite loop and freeze the network. Gas prices every operation — a simple transfer costs 21,000 gas, storage writes cost far more — and transactions carry a gas limit that halts runaway execution. The fee pays validators for real work and makes spam economically painful. Keep this frame for the next lesson; gas is a resource market, not a fee the app chose." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Classify the participants', instructions: ['List three addresses a token swap involves: the swapper, the token contract, the DEX pool contract.', 'For each, label it EOA or contract account.', 'Write one sentence on who initiated the transaction.', 'Note who pays the gas.'], solution: 'The swapper is an EOA — only a private key can sign and start a transaction. The token and pool contracts are contract accounts; they execute when called. The swapper’s signature initiates the call chain, and the swapper pays all gas consumed by every contract invoked.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Who can initiate a state change on Ethereum?', options: ['Any smart contract, on its own schedule', 'Only an externally owned account, by signing a transaction', 'Any validator that produces the next block'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Contracts never run unprompted — they execute only when a transaction (or a call rooted in one) reaches them. Every call chain traces back to an EOA signature.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Name the fields inside an Ethereum transaction.', 'Explain gas limit vs gas price and how fees are computed.', 'Read the key fields of a transaction receipt.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Anatomy of a transaction', text: "A transaction is a signed instruction with a handful of fields. nonce counts your prior transactions and prevents replays. to is the destination — an EOA for a plain transfer, or a contract for an interaction. value is ETH sent; data carries the function call for contracts. gasLimit caps work; maxFeePerGas bounds what you will pay. Your wallet fills most of this; you should still recognize each field when you inspect one on a block explorer.", code: "{\n  nonce: 41,\n  to: '0xToken…',\n  value: 0,\n  data: '0xa9059cbb…',\n  gasLimit: 65000,\n  maxFeePerGas: 30 gwei,\n  maxPriorityFeePerGas: 2 gwei\n}", language: 'json' },
        { title: 'Gas: limit, price, and the burn', text: "Think of gas like a taxi meter: gasLimit is the maximum ride you authorize — run out mid-execution and the transaction fails but the meter is still owed. Since EIP-1559 each block has a base fee, set by protocol and burned, plus a priority tip to the validator. Your effective fee is gasUsed × (baseFee + tip), capped by your maxFeePerGas. Simple transfer = 21,000 gas; contract calls vary with the work done.", code: "fee = gasUsed * (baseFee + priorityTip)\nexample: 21,000 gas * (25 + 2) gwei = 567,000 gwei = 0.000567 ETH", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Reading a receipt', text: "A receipt answers three questions: did it succeed (status 1 or 0), how much gas it used, and what the contract emitted via logs. For a token transfer, logs contain the Transfer event with from, to, and amount — events are how apps and indexers observe contract activity. Get fluent reading receipts on a testnet explorer now; it is the single most useful debugging habit in Ethereum development.", code: "status: '0x1'              // success\ngasUsed: 46,230\nlogs: [\n  Transfer(from: you, to: contract, value: 0.01 ETH)\n]", language: 'json' },
        { title: 'Pending, failed, and dropped', text: "Broadcast transactions sit in the mempool until a validator includes them. A stuck transaction usually means a gas bid below current demand — speed it up by resubmitting with the same nonce and higher fee. A failed (reverted) transaction still costs the gas it consumed: the network did the work before hitting your revert. And a low-fee transaction can be dropped entirely from mempools; it never happened, and nothing was spent." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Decode a receipt', instructions: ['Find any transaction on Sepolia or another testnet explorer.', 'Record its status, gasUsed, and effective gas price.', 'Compute the fee in ETH from those two numbers.', 'List the events in its logs and name what each records.'], solution: 'Fee = gasUsed × effectiveGasPrice. If gasUsed is 46,230 and the effective price is 27 gwei, the fee is 1,248,210 gwei = 0.00124821 ETH. A token transfer shows a Transfer event with from/to/amount; a swap shows Swap events from the pool contract.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Your transaction reverted after using 40,000 gas. What do you pay?', options: ['Nothing — failed transactions are free', 'The full fee for the 40,000 gas consumed', 'A flat penalty set by the protocol'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Validators executed real computation before the revert. Gas pays for work done, not for success — the fee is still charged.' },
    },
  ],
  'stellar-network-development': [
    {
      objectives: ['Describe a Stellar account and its required fields.', 'Distinguish native XLM from issued assets defined by code and issuer.', 'Explain the ledger and base reserve model.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Accounts: a public key with state', text: "A Stellar account is an entry in a shared ledger: a public address (starting with G), a sequence number, balances, and optional subentries like trustlines and signers. There is no registration authority — a keypair you generate locally becomes an account the moment it is funded. Accounts must hold a small minimum XLM balance called the base reserve, which keeps the ledger free of dust spam.", code: "Account (simplified):\n  publicKey:  GABC...XYZ\n  sequence:   12884901889\n  balances:   [ XLM: 25.5, USDC: 100.0 (via trustline) ]\n  subentries: trustlines, offers, signers", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Assets: XLM vs issued tokens', text: "XLM is native — it exists by protocol rule, pays fees, and funds reserves. Everything else is an issued asset identified by two parts: a code (USDC, EURC) plus its issuer account. 'USDC' alone is ambiguous — anyone could issue a token labeled USDC — so the issuer matters. Circle's USDC is USDC issued from Circle's known address. Wallets display code + issuer together for exactly this reason.", code: "Asset identity = code + issuer\n  XLM                 -> native, no issuer\n  USDC:GA5Z...CIRCLE  -> Circle's official asset\n  USDC:GF4K...RANDOM  -> someone else's token, same code", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'The ledger: one shared state', text: "Stellar keeps a single ledger — a snapshot of every account, trustline, offer, and contract entry — plus the transaction history that produced it. Each ledger closes in about five seconds, applying a batch of transactions chosen by consensus. Unlike UTXO chains that track coins, Stellar tracks account state directly, which makes balances and asset holdings trivial to query through Horizon." },
        { title: 'Reserves and rent economics', text: "Every account needs a small XLM base reserve, and each trustline, open offer, signer, or contract data entry adds to it. Reserves are locked, not spent — remove the subentry and the reserve frees. The model prices ledger space honestly: permanent state has a real cost, which is why applications batch and clean up rather than sprawl. Keep this in mind when designing anything that creates per-user subentries." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Read an account', instructions: ['Open the Stellar testnet explorer or laboratory.', 'Create and fund a test account with Friendbot.', 'Record its public key, sequence number, and starting balance.', 'Write down the minimum balance shown and name what creates it.'], solution: 'A fresh Friendbot account shows a starting balance (typically 10,000 test XLM on testnet), sequence number 1 higher than the funding ledger, and a minimum balance equal to the base reserve for a single subentry-free account. The reserve exists to prevent ledger spam, not as a fee.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What uniquely identifies a USDC asset on Stellar?', options: ['The code “USDC”', 'The code plus the issuing account', 'The wallet that holds it'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Anyone can issue a token labeled USDC. Asset identity is code + issuer — which is why wallets and applications always check the issuing address.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Describe a Stellar transaction as a signed container of operations.', 'Name the most common operation types.', 'Explain atomic execution and why it matters for payments.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Transactions wrap operations', text: "A Stellar transaction is an envelope: it carries a source account, a sequence number, a fee, signatures, and a list of one or more operations — the actual work. A transaction can hold up to 100 operations, which is how Stellar batches multi-step payments atomically. The whole envelope either applies or fails; partial execution is impossible.", code: "Transaction\n  source: GABC...\n  seq:    12884901890\n  fee:    100 stroops\n  ops:    [ Payment, ChangeTrust, ... ]\n  signatures: [ sig(by source key) ]", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Operations you will meet daily', text: "CreateAccount funds a new address with starting XLM. Payment sends native or issued assets. ChangeTrust adds or removes a trustline. ManageSellOffer places an order on the built-in DEX, and PathPaymentStrictSend converts across assets mid-payment. Learning to think in operations — not 'send money' as one verb — is the mental shift that unlocks Stellar's design." },
        { title: 'Atomicity for free', text: "Because operations inside a transaction apply atomically, Stellar does compound payments natively: 'create the recipient's trustline, then pay them USDC, then record a memo' is one transaction — all of it or none. On platforms without this, developers simulate atomicity with escrow contracts or multi-call patterns. Atomic batches are a genuine advantage for payment products where partial failure means reconciliation work." },
        { title: 'Fees, sequence, and signing', text: "Fees are tiny by design — base fee is measured in stroops — and scale with operation count and network demand. The sequence number must equal last-sequence + 1, which prevents replays and orders each account's transactions. Every transaction needs the required signers; Stellar supports multi-signature natively, so an ops account can require two-of-three approvals before anything moves." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Compose a two-operation transaction', instructions: ['Using the Stellar Laboratory on testnet, build a transaction with a ChangeTrust (USDC) followed by a Payment of USDC to a second funded test account.', 'Sign with the source secret and submit.', 'Fetch the transaction from Horizon and record whether both operations applied together.', 'Predict what happens if the payment account is unfunded — then test your prediction.'], solution: 'Both operations apply or neither does: an unfunded destination account causes the Payment to fail, which fails the whole envelope — the trustline change rolls back too. Verify on Horizon: the failed transaction returns a result code (op_no_destination or similar) and the ledger state shows no trustline was added.' },
      quiz: { question: 'A transaction contains a ChangeTrust and a Payment; the Payment fails. What happens to the ChangeTrust?', options: ['It applies — each operation is independent', 'It rolls back — the transaction is atomic', 'It stays pending until retried'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Stellar transactions are atomic containers. One failed operation fails the envelope; nothing in it is applied to the ledger.' },
    },
  ],
  'zero-knowledge-proofs': [
    {
      objectives: ['Explain a zero-knowledge proof in plain language.', 'Give two real-world examples of prove-without-revealing.', 'Distinguish proving a statement from revealing the evidence behind it.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'The cave story', text: "Imagine a circular cave with a magic door deep inside that opens only for a secret word. You claim to know the word; a verifier waits outside, then calls out which exit you should emerge from — left or right. If you really know the word, you can always take the hidden path and come out the side called. If you are guessing, half the time you are trapped on the wrong side. Repeat twenty rounds and a faker's survival odds drop below one in a million — yet the verifier never heard the word. That is a zero-knowledge proof: convincing evidence with zero information leak.", code: "Round i:\n  prover enters cave, picks a side at random\n  verifier calls 'left!' or 'right!'\n  honest prover -> always exits correctly\n  cheater     -> caught with probability 1/2 per round\n  20 rounds   -> cheat succeeds 1 in ~1,000,000", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'What is actually being proven', text: "A ZKP proves a statement, not data. 'I am over 18' is provable without showing your birthdate. 'I know the preimage of this hash' is provable without showing the preimage. 'This transaction batch was computed correctly' is provable without re-executing it. The statement is public; the witness — the secret evidence satisfying it — stays private. Keeping those two roles straight is the core skill of thinking in ZK." },
        { title: 'Why blockchains care', text: "Blockchains solve agreement, not privacy — every node re-executes everything and sees everything. ZKPs offer two escapes: privacy (prove a payment is valid without revealing sender, amount, or recipient — Zcash's model) and compression (prove ten thousand transactions were executed correctly so nodes verify one small proof instead — the zk-rollup model). Same primitive, different superpower." },
        { title: 'Prover, verifier, and cost', text: "Two roles exist and their costs are wildly asymmetric. The prover does heavy computation — generating a proof can take seconds to minutes and real hardware. The verifier checks a small proof in milliseconds, cheaply enough to run inside a smart contract. That asymmetry is why ZK works on-chain: one party sweats, everyone else checks cheaply. Keep the roles in mind whenever someone says 'ZK is expensive' — ask expensive for whom." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Design a proof statement', instructions: ['Pick a real claim: “I am a citizen of country X” or “I own this credential.”', 'Write the public statement a verifier should check.', 'Write the private witness that must stay hidden.', 'List one thing your statement deliberately does not prove.'], solution: 'Example — “I am over 18”: public statement = my birthdate attested by issuer Y is more than 18 years before today; witness = the birthdate itself and the signed credential. It does not prove which exact birthdate, or which country issued the credential — only the threshold claim. Deliberately scoping the statement is the design work.' },
      quiz: { question: 'In a zero-knowledge proof, what does the verifier learn?', options: ['The secret plus proof it is true', 'Only that the statement is true', 'A hint that can be combined over many proofs'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Zero-knowledge means exactly that: the verifier gains confidence in the statement and nothing about the witness. Properly constructed proofs leak no information even across many rounds.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['State the three properties every ZKP must satisfy.', 'Distinguish interactive from non-interactive proofs.', 'Explain why verifiability, not secrecy alone, is the practical breakthrough.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Completeness: truth convinces', text: "If the statement is true and both parties behave honestly, the verifier accepts. Completeness sounds trivial but constrains design: a proof system that rejects valid proofs — or needs conditions the prover cannot always meet — fails real deployments. Think of it as the no-false-negatives guarantee." },
        { title: 'Soundness: lies cannot fake it', text: "If the statement is false, no cheating prover can convince the verifier — except with negligible probability. Soundness is the property everything hinges on: break it and forged proofs pass as real, which on a blockchain means printing money or bypassing rules. Most ZK engineering is, at heart, defending soundness under weird inputs and adversarial conditions.", code: "The three properties, one line each:\n  completeness:   true statement  -> verifier accepts\n  soundness:      false statement -> verifier rejects (whp)\n  zero-knowledge: verifier learns nothing beyond 'true'", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Interactive vs non-interactive', text: "The cave protocol is interactive — prover and verifier exchange challenges live. Blockchains cannot wait for that: a smart contract must verify a proof left on-chain with no back-and-forth. Non-interactive proofs (NIZKs) package the challenge into the proof itself, typically using a hash of the transcript as the random challenge (the Fiat–Shamir transform). Nearly every ZK system you will meet — SNARK, STARK, Bulletproof — is non-interactive for this reason." },
        { title: 'The real breakthrough: cheap verification', text: "Secrecy alone would be a niche feature. The revolution is that verification is exponentially cheaper than re-execution: a smart contract can verify '100,000 transactions were processed correctly' from a proof smaller than a page of text, in milliseconds, for cents. That is the entire premise of zk-rollups — compressing computation into proofs — and why ZK shifted from privacy research to scaling infrastructure." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Classify these claims', instructions: ['Claim A: a system where honest proofs sometimes fail verification.', 'Claim B: a system where a forged proof passed once in 10^9 attempts.', 'Claim C: a proof that reveals which of two secrets the prover knows.', 'For each, name which property breaks.'], solution: 'A breaks completeness — true statements must always verify. B technically still satisfies soundness if the probability is genuinely negligible (soundness allows negligible error), though whether 10^-9 is acceptable depends on the threat model. C breaks zero-knowledge — the proof leaked information about the witness.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Why do blockchains need non-interactive proofs?', options: ['Interactive proofs are insecure', 'A verifier contract cannot exchange live challenges with a prover', 'Non-interactive proofs are faster to generate'], answer: 1, explanation: 'On-chain verification is asynchronous — the proof sits in a transaction and any node must verify it independently, with no dialogue. NIZKs embed the challenge inside the proof itself.' },
    },
  ],
  'crypto-fundamentals': [
    {
      objectives: ['Explain the double-spend problem in plain terms.', 'Describe what a ledger is and why central ones need trust.', 'State what blockchains add to digital money.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Money is a ledger', text: "Most money is already digital — your bank balance is a number in a database the bank maintains. A ledger is simply the record of who has what and what changed. Banks work because everyone trusts the bank to keep that ledger honestly: debits match credits, nobody spends the same dollar twice, and the record survives. Digital money before Bitcoin could not escape needing that trusted operator." },
        { title: 'The double-spend problem', text: "A digital coin is data — and data copies perfectly. If Alice pays the same coin to Bob and to Carol, and no central arbiter exists, who decides which payment counts? For decades this made decentralized digital cash seem impossible: without a referee, every participant could claim conflicting histories. This is the problem Satoshi Nakamoto's 2008 paper actually solved.", code: "Without a shared ledger:\n  Alice -> Bob   (pays coin #7)\n  Alice -> Carol (pays coin #7 again)\n  Bob says paid, Carol says paid — who is right?", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'The blockchain answer', text: "A blockchain makes the ledger itself the referee. Thousands of nodes keep identical copies; consensus rules decide which transactions enter the shared history, and cryptographic linking makes past entries tamper-evident. Alice's second spend of coin #7 is simply rejected — the history already shows it spent. No bank required; the trust moves from an institution to a protocol anyone can audit." },
        { title: 'What you are really holding', text: "When you 'own crypto' you own an entry in that shared ledger — a balance attributed to an address, controlled by a key. Nothing sits in your wallet app; the wallet holds the key that can authorize changes to your ledger entries. This mental model — ledger, address, key — is the foundation every later lesson builds on, and the reason 'not your keys, not your coins' is more than a slogan." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Trace the ledger', instructions: ['Write the three-party scenario: Alice pays Bob 5 coins, Bob pays Carol 3.', 'Show the ledger entries after each payment starting from Alice = 10, Bob = 0, Carol = 0.', 'Now try Alice paying Bob AND Carol 8 each — what should a correct ledger reject?', 'Write one sentence on who rejected it in a bank model vs a blockchain model.'], solution: 'After payment one: Alice 5, Bob 5. After two: Bob 2, Carol 3. The double 8-payment must be rejected — Alice only has 10 total. A bank rejects it because its central ledger shows insufficient funds; a blockchain rejects it because every node applies the same rule to the same shared history.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What problem did Bitcoin’s design solve?', options: ['Making digital files copyable', 'Preventing double-spending without a central operator', 'Encrypting money so banks cannot see it'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The double-spend problem — one digital coin spent twice — previously required a trusted intermediary. A consensus-secured shared ledger removes that requirement.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Relate private keys, public keys, addresses, and seed phrases.', 'Explain what signing a transaction proves.', 'State the rules for protecting a seed phrase.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'One direction only', text: "A private key is a giant random number you must keep secret. From it, math derives a public key — anyone can verify it came from your private key but nobody can reverse the derivation. An address is a shortened, checksummed form of the public key, safe to share — it is how people pay you. The pipeline is strictly one-way: private → public → address, never backward.", code: "private key (secret, random)  --derive-->  public key  --hash-->  address\nshare:  address\nnever:  private key, seed phrase", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Signing is proving, not encrypting', text: "Sending crypto means signing a transaction: your private key produces a signature over the transaction data, and every node verifies it against your public key — proof that the key-holder authorized exactly this payment, without ever revealing the key. Nothing is encrypted; the signature is a proof of authorization. This is why stolen signatures replay nothing (they bind to one message) and stolen keys steal everything." },
        { title: 'Seed phrases: the master backup', text: "Modern wallets derive all your private keys from one secret encoded as 12 or 24 words — the seed phrase (BIP-39). Anyone holding it regenerates your keys and drains every account it made. Treat it accordingly: write it on paper or steel, store it offline in a safe place, never photograph, email, or cloud-sync it, and never type it into a website. No legitimate service — wallet, exchange, or 'support' — will ever ask for it." },
        { title: 'Custody is a spectrum', text: "Custodial services (exchanges, apps) hold keys for you: convenient recovery, but you trust them not to lose, freeze, or steal funds — 'not your keys, not your coins.' Non-custodial wallets give you the keys and the full responsibility: lose the seed phrase and there is no password reset. Most losses happen at this layer — key management — not through cryptography being broken." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Classify the secrets', instructions: ['Sort into SHARE vs NEVER-SHARE: address, private key, seed phrase, public key, transaction signature.', 'Write the rule you would give a friend who asks where to store their seed phrase.', 'Describe what an attacker can do with each NEVER-SHARE item.', 'Explain why the signature is safe even though it derives from the private key.'], solution: 'SHARE: address, public key, signature (each proves or receives without exposing the key). NEVER-SHARE: private key and seed phrase — either one allows full control of funds. The signature is safe because it is bound to one specific transaction and reveals nothing about the key that produced it.' },
      quiz: { question: 'A stranger from “wallet support” asks for your seed phrase to fix a sync issue. What do you do?', options: ['Share it — support needs it to resync your wallet', 'Refuse — no legitimate service ever needs your seed phrase', 'Share only the first six words to be safe'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The seed phrase is total control of your funds. Anyone asking for it is attempting theft — full stop. Even partial phrases materially weaken your security.' },
    },
  ],
  'bitcoin-fundamentals': [
    {
      objectives: ['Explain the double-spend problem Bitcoin solved.', 'Describe Bitcoin as digital scarcity without an issuer.', 'Distinguish BTC the asset from Bitcoin the network.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'The double-spend problem', text: "Digital files copy perfectly — that is why digital cash was impossible for decades: any 'coin' could be spent twice. Banks solved it by keeping the only ledger and charging for the privilege. Bitcoin's breakthrough in 2008 was a ledger no single party controls — thousands of nodes enforce one shared history, making double-spending impractical without trusting anyone in particular." },
        { title: 'Digital scarcity, finally', text: "Bitcoin's rules cap supply at 21 million coins, enforced by code every node runs rather than a promise from an issuer. That fixed, predictable scarcity is the core of the 'digital gold' narrative: like gold, nobody can print more; unlike gold, it can be sent anywhere in minutes. Whether that makes it money, a store of value, or a speculative asset is a live debate — the scarcity mechanism itself is not." },
        { title: 'BTC the asset vs Bitcoin the network', text: "Bitcoin (capital B) is the network and protocol; bitcoin or BTC (lowercase) is the asset that lives on it — divisible to 8 decimals, with the smallest unit called a satoshi (100 million sats = 1 BTC). The distinction matters when reading claims: the network has been extraordinarily reliable as engineered, while the asset's price has been wildly volatile. Both statements can be true at once.", code: "1 BTC = 100,000,000 satoshis\nmax supply: 21,000,000 BTC (hard-coded)\nnew issuance: block reward, halves ~every 4 years", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'What it is not', text: "Honest framing matters: Bitcoin is not a company (no CEO to call), not backed by anything physical (its value is market-set), not anonymous (pseudonymous — transactions are public), and not risk-free (price swings, custody responsibility, regulatory uncertainty). Understanding what it is not prevents most beginner mistakes before any money is involved." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Explain it to a friend', instructions: ['Write a two-sentence explanation of the double-spend problem.', 'State the supply cap and who enforces it.', 'Explain the BTC-asset vs Bitcoin-network distinction.', 'List two things Bitcoin is NOT that beginners often assume.'], solution: 'Double-spend: digital items copy freely, so digital cash needs a shared record nobody can rewrite — Bitcoin provides it without a central operator. Cap: 21 million, enforced by every node’s consensus rules, not an issuer’s promise. Network vs asset: the network is the infrastructure; BTC is the scarce token on it. Common wrong assumptions: backed by gold/dollars, run by a company, fully anonymous, guaranteed returns.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Why was digital cash impossible before Bitcoin?', options: ['Computers were too slow', 'Digital files copy perfectly — nothing stopped spending the same coin twice without a trusted central ledger', 'The internet had no payment standards'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Copyable files mean copyable money. Bitcoin solved double-spending with a public ledger all honest nodes enforce — no trusted party required.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Describe how blocks form a chain.', 'Explain the UTXO model vs account balances.', 'Read a Bitcoin transaction’s inputs and outputs.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Blocks and the chain', text: "Bitcoin batches transactions into blocks roughly every ten minutes. Each block contains a cryptographic hash of the previous block — the chain — so altering an old transaction changes every hash after it, which honest nodes instantly reject. Immutability is not magic; it is math plus thousands of independent verifiers who refuse to accept edited history." },
        { title: 'UTXOs: digital cash, literally', text: "Bitcoin has no 'account balances' — it has unspent transaction outputs (UTXOs): discrete chunks of BTC locked to addresses, like bills in a physical wallet. Your wallet balance is just the sum of UTXOs you can unlock. Spending consumes whole UTXOs and creates new ones — pay 0.6 of a 1.0 UTXO and the protocol creates a 0.6 output to the recipient plus a ~0.4 'change' output back to you.", code: "TX inputs:   [1.0 BTC utxo you own]\nTX outputs:  [0.6 BTC -> recipient] [0.3995 BTC -> your change]\nfee:         0.0005 BTC (input - outputs, goes to miner)", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Reading a transaction', text: "Every transaction lists inputs (UTXOs being consumed, each unlocked with a signature) and outputs (new UTXOs being created). The difference between total input and total output is the miner fee — there is no explicit fee field. Explorers show this clearly; learning to read inputs/outputs demystifies where money goes and why fees vary." },
        { title: 'Why it matters later', text: "The UTXO model shapes real behavior: wallets merge small UTXOs (raising future fees), exchanges batch payments into single transactions, and privacy tools exploit input patterns. Ethereum's account model — a simple balance per address — trades some of Bitcoin's clean parallelism for easier smart contracts. Neither is wrong; they optimize different things." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Trace a transaction', instructions: ['Given a 2.0 BTC UTXO and a 0.75 BTC payment, write the inputs and outputs including change and a 0.001 fee.', 'Explain why there is no fee field in a transaction.', 'Find any transaction on a Bitcoin testnet explorer and identify its inputs/outputs.', 'Contrast UTXOs with an account balance in one sentence.'], solution: 'Inputs: [2.0]. Outputs: [0.75 → recipient], [1.249 → your change]. Fee = 2.0 − (0.75 + 1.249) = 0.001. The fee is implicit — miners collect the unclaimed difference. Account model tracks a number per address; UTXO tracks discrete spendable outputs, like cash vs a bank balance.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What is a Bitcoin UTXO?', options: ['A type of Bitcoin address', 'A discrete chunk of spendable bitcoin — inputs consume UTXOs and outputs create new ones', 'A mining reward'], answer: 1, explanation: 'UTXOs are the actual units of ownership — your balance is their sum. Transactions destroy old UTXOs as inputs and create new ones as outputs, change included.' },
    },
  ],
  'chatgpt-essentials': [
    {
      objectives: ['Explain what a large language model does.', 'Set up ChatGPT and its key settings.', 'State what ChatGPT cannot reliably do.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'A prediction engine, not a database', text: "ChatGPT is a large language model: it generates text by predicting what should come next, trained on enormous text corpora. It does not look facts up in a database — it produces plausible continuations. That explains both its fluency and its failure mode: confident, well-phrased errors called hallucinations. Every workflow in this course is built around getting value from prediction while verifying the parts that must be true." },
        { title: 'The product, not the model', text: "ChatGPT the app wraps the underlying model in a conversational interface, memory, tools (search, file reading, image understanding, code execution), and account features like custom GPTs. When people compare 'ChatGPT vs Claude' they usually mean the whole product experience — model plus tools plus UX — not just raw model quality." },
        { title: 'Setup and settings that matter', text: "Create a free account at chatgpt.com, then check three settings: memory (whether it remembers preferences across chats), data controls (whether your chats train models), and custom instructions (standing context about you and your preferred style). A few minutes here shapes every later conversation — set custom instructions to describe your role and how you want answers formatted." },
        { title: 'The honest limits', text: "ChatGPT cannot guarantee facts (verify anything you would cite), cannot know things after its cutoff without browsing, cannot access your private files unless you upload them, and should not receive secrets — API keys, passwords, confidential documents. Treat it like a brilliant intern with amnesia and occasional confident lies: delegate drafting and thinking, keep verification and judgment." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Set up deliberately', instructions: ['Create or open your ChatGPT account and find memory, data controls, and custom instructions.', 'Write custom instructions describing your role and preferred answer format.', 'Ask one question with a verifiable answer and check it against a primary source.', 'Write one thing you would NOT paste into ChatGPT and why.'], solution: 'Settings located under profile → Settings (Personalization/Memory, Data controls, Custom instructions). Good custom instructions name your role, domain, and style (“senior dev, prefer concise answers with code”). Verification check: pick a question with a definite answer (e.g., a date or API name) and confirm it on an official source. Never paste credentials, private keys, or confidential client data.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Why does ChatGPT sometimes state wrong facts confidently?', options: ['It is programmed to deceive', 'It predicts plausible text rather than looking up verified facts — hallucination is inherent to prediction', 'It only makes mistakes on old topics'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The model generates what likely comes next, not what is true. Fluency and accuracy are correlated, not identical — which is why verification habits matter.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Structure a prompt with context, task, and constraints.', 'Use examples and iteration to improve output.', 'Debug a failing prompt.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'The three-part prompt', text: "Reliable prompts have three parts: context (who you are, what this is for, relevant background), task (the specific thing you want done), and constraints (format, length, tone, what to avoid). 'Write a summary' produces generic mush; 'Summarize this report for a non-technical VP in 5 bullets, leading with the risk' produces something usable on the first try. Specificity is not extra work — it is the work." },
        { title: 'Show, do not just tell', text: "Examples beat descriptions. If you want output in a particular style or format, paste one or two examples of it — few-shot prompting. 'Here is how I write release notes: [example]. Write the next one for these changes: [...]' consistently outperforms ten sentences describing your style. The model imitates structure it can see." },
        { title: 'Iterate like a conversation, not a slot machine', text: "The first output is a draft, not a verdict. Iterate with specific feedback: 'shorter', 'more technical', 'remove the jargon', 'that table should compare costs not features.' Each round costs seconds and compounds — three rounds of feedback typically beat one elaborately perfect prompt. And ask the model to critique its own output; self-review catches obvious misses." },
        { title: 'Debugging prompts that fail', text: "When output disappoints, diagnose before retrying: missing context (the model cannot know your situation unless told), ambiguous task (two reasonable readings — it picked one), missing constraints (got an essay, wanted a table), or asking beyond the model (needs real-time data or your private files). Rewrite the prompt fixing the actual gap rather than rephrasing and hoping.", code: "Weak:   'Write about our product.'\nBetter: 'You are a B2B marketer. Write a 150-word LinkedIn post\n        announcing our reporting feature for finance managers.\n        Tone: confident, no jargon. End with one CTA to the demo page.'", language: 'text' },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Upgrade a weak prompt', instructions: ['Take this weak prompt: “Write something about our product launch.” Rewrite it with context, task, and constraints.', 'Add a one-line example of the style you want.', 'Run both versions and compare outputs in one paragraph.', 'List two failure modes you saw and how your rewrite prevented them.'], solution: 'A strong rewrite names the audience, purpose, format, length, tone, and exclusions — e.g., “For a newsletter to existing customers, write 100 words announcing our CSV export feature; plain language; end with “Try it” CTA; no hype words like “revolutionary”.” The comparison typically shows the weak version producing generic filler while the constrained version is directly usable.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What most improves a prompt’s output?', options: ['Using longer, fancier vocabulary', 'Adding specific context, task definition, and output constraints', 'Asking the same question repeatedly'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Models respond to specificity: knowing your goal, audience, and format requirements produces targeted output — generic asks get generic answers.' },
    },
  ],
  'claude-mastery': [
    {
      objectives: ['Describe Claude and the Anthropic model family.', 'Identify the tasks where Claude excels.', 'Set up a Claude account and projects.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Claude and Anthropic', text: "Claude is Anthropic's AI assistant — built by a company founded around AI safety. The model family tiers by capability and speed; the flagship models trade higher cost for stronger reasoning, especially on nuanced writing, careful analysis, and code. Product-wise, Claude lives at claude.ai and via API, with features like Projects, Artifacts, and a large context window as its distinguishing strengths." },
        { title: 'Where Claude shines', text: "Claude's reputation rests on three strengths: long documents (it digests books, contracts, and codebases within its context window), calibrated writing (it follows style instructions precisely and hedges appropriately rather than overclaiming), and code work (clean explanations and edits). If your work involves reading more than generating, Claude is often the strongest choice among assistants." },
        { title: 'Context window as a feature', text: "The context window is how much text the model can consider at once — Claude's is among the largest. Practically, that means you can paste an entire report or several files and ask cross-document questions without chunking. Large context does not mean perfect recall — important details still get missed mid-document, which is why later lessons cover verification." },
        { title: 'Setup notes', text: "Sign up at claude.ai, then learn the two features that shape daily use: Projects (persistent workspaces holding your documents and instructions) and Artifacts (a side panel where Claude drafts documents, code, or visuals you iterate on together). Free tier covers fundamentals; heavy document work may hit usage limits — plan big analyses accordingly." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Strengths audit', instructions: ['List your three most common assistant tasks.', 'For each, predict whether Claude is a strong fit based on its strengths (documents, writing, code) — and why.', 'Create a Claude account and open the Projects and Artifacts panels.', 'Write one sentence on when you would pick another tool.'], solution: 'Document analysis, careful editing, and code review fit Claude best; live web search needs, image generation, or deep integration with a specific ecosystem (Google/Microsoft) favor alternatives. The deliverable is a mental routing table: you should know which assistant you reach for and why before the course ends.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What is Claude’s most distinctive capability?', options: ['Generating images', 'Working with very long documents inside a large context window', 'Real-time stock quotes'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Claude’s large context window lets it reason over entire books, contracts, and codebases — its signature strength alongside careful writing.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Prompt Claude with structure and examples.', 'Use XML-style tags to organize inputs.', 'Iterate on Claude output effectively.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Clarity over cleverness', text: "Claude responds exceptionally well to plainly stated structure: what you want, in what format, for whom, and what to avoid. Anthropic's own guidance favors explicit instructions over tricks — tell Claude to think step by step when reasoning matters, and say exactly what the output should look like. Vague prompts produce vague essays; precise prompts produce usable drafts." },
        { title: 'XML tags for structure', text: "A Claude-specific habit: wrap inputs in XML-style tags — <document>, <example>, <instructions>. Tags let the model distinguish your instructions from the material being processed, which matters enormously with long inputs. When pasting a report to summarize, <report>...</report> plus clear instructions beats an undifferentiated wall of text every time.", code: "<document>\n[paste the contract here]\n</document>\n\n<task>\nList every clause that creates a payment obligation,\nwith the section number and a one-line plain-English summary.\n</task>", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Examples and style anchors', text: "Like every LLM, Claude imitates what it sees. To get your voice or format, paste an example inside <example> tags and ask for more of the same. For editing tasks, give it the text plus your style rules as a short checklist — Claude follows enumerated constraints unusually well, so list them rather than embedding them in prose." },
        { title: 'Iterating with Claude', text: "Use follow-ups surgically: 'keep the structure, tighten paragraph two', 'make the tone more formal', 'expand the third bullet with an example.' Claude maintains context well within a conversation, so iterative refinement is cheap. For bigger pivots, start a new chat — a fresh context beats dragging a polluted thread forward." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Tag your input', instructions: ['Paste a real document into Claude wrapped in <document> tags with a specific extraction task.', 'Compare the tagged prompt against the same request without tags.', 'Add one <example> of your desired output format and rerun.', 'Note which version produced the most usable result and why.'], solution: 'Tagged prompts consistently outperform untagged ones on document tasks because the model cleanly separates instructions from content. The example-driven version should match your format almost exactly — that is the pattern to reuse: tags for structure, examples for style.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Why wrap pasted documents in XML tags?', options: ['Claude requires XML syntax', 'Tags separate instructions from content — the model knows exactly what to process versus what to do', 'It makes documents shorter'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Tags create explicit boundaries so Claude treats tagged text as input material and untagged text as instructions — critical for long-document accuracy.' },
    },
  ],
  'gemini-essentials': [
    {
      objectives: ['Describe Gemini and its place in Google’s ecosystem.', 'Access Gemini across web, mobile, and Workspace.', 'Identify Gemini’s distinguishing features.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Gemini the product', text: "Gemini is Google's AI assistant — a family of models plus the product built on them at gemini.google.com, in the mobile app, and embedded across Google Workspace (Docs, Gmail, Drive, Sheets). Its defining advantage is not raw conversation but integration: it can work where your files and email already live, and it was built multimodal from the start — text, images, video frames, and audio as first-class inputs." },
        { title: 'Where it fits', text: "If your workday runs on Google — Gmail, Docs, Drive — Gemini's embedded presence changes the calculus: summarizing a thread in Gmail or drafting inside Docs beats copy-pasting to a separate app. It also offers Deep Research (multi-step web research reports) and a very large context window on premium tiers. If you live outside Google's ecosystem, its advantages shrink to model capability alone." },
        { title: 'Access and tiers', text: "A free Google account gets you the web and mobile apps with everyday features; paid tiers unlock the strongest models, Workspace side-panel integration, and larger context. Google also rebranded its developer models under Gemini (AI Studio, Vertex AI) — the same family at different depths, from casual chat to API production." },
        { title: 'The honest trade-offs', text: "Gemini's strengths come with caveats: Workspace features need the paid tier; answers still require verification like every LLM; and deep integration means trusting Google with more of your workflow — review the data settings. For users already all-in on Google it is often the highest-leverage assistant to learn; for others it is a strong multimodal option among several." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Map your Google surface', instructions: ['List the Google apps you use daily (Gmail, Docs, Drive, Sheets, Meet).', 'For each, note one task Gemini could assist.', 'Open gemini.google.com and locate Workspace features available on your tier.', 'Write one sentence on whether the free tier covers your expected use.'], solution: 'Typical wins: summarizing email threads, drafting Docs, organizing Drive, formula help in Sheets, meeting notes. Whether free suffices depends on Workspace side-panel needs — many core chat features work free, but in-app Workspace assistance generally requires a paid plan.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What is Gemini’s main advantage over standalone assistants?', options: ['It is the only multimodal model', 'Deep integration with Google Workspace — it works inside Docs, Gmail, and Drive where your files already live', 'It never makes mistakes'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Ecosystem fit is Gemini’s differentiator: if your files and email live in Google, embedded assistance beats copy-pasting into a separate app.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Send multimodal prompts to Gemini.', 'Use images, files, and mixed input effectively.', 'Evaluate multimodal output critically.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Multimodal by design', text: "Gemini was built multimodal rather than bolting vision on later — you can upload images, screenshots, PDFs, and even video or audio and ask questions across them. Practical uses: explain a chart, extract text from a photo, compare two screenshots, summarize a slide deck, transcribe handwritten notes. The prompt pattern stays the same — attach, then ask something specific." },
        { title: 'Asking the right multimodal questions', text: "Multimodal prompts work best when specific: not 'what is this?' but 'extract the totals from this invoice photo into a table' or 'which of these two UI screenshots has the clearer navigation and why?' Vague questions on images get generic descriptions; task-shaped questions get usable answers." },
        { title: 'Documents and long inputs', text: "Gemini handles large uploads — long PDFs, slide decks, multiple files — and on premium tiers offers an exceptionally large context window. That makes it competitive for the same long-document work as Claude and Kimi. Same discipline applies: ask targeted questions, and verify critical claims against the source since long-context recall can still miss details." },
        { title: 'Verify what it sees', text: "Vision models make their own hallucination class: confidently misreading text, mislabeling chart elements, or inventing details in blurry images. For anything high-stakes — numbers, legal text, UI comparisons — cross-check the output against the image yourself. The skill is using multimodal speed for extraction while keeping verification for accuracy-critical items." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Multimodal drill', instructions: ['Upload a photo of a document, receipt, or chart to Gemini.', 'Ask for structured extraction (a table of the key fields).', 'Compare the extraction to the original — mark every error.', 'Try one vague prompt on the same image and note the difference in usefulness.'], solution: 'Specific extraction requests (totals, dates, line items as a table) should produce mostly correct structured output with occasional transcription errors — catchable by eyeballing the source. The vague prompt will produce a generic description, illustrating why task-shaped multimodal prompts matter.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What makes a multimodal prompt effective?', options: ['Uploading higher-resolution files only', 'Asking a specific, task-shaped question about the attached content', 'Using technical vocabulary'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The model can see the attachment either way — specificity of the ask determines usefulness. “Extract the totals” beats “what is this?” every time.' },
    },
  ],
  'deepseek-practice': [
    {
      objectives: ['Describe DeepSeek and the open-weights model family.', 'Explain why open weights matter.', 'Access DeepSeek via app and API.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'What DeepSeek is', text: "DeepSeek is a Chinese AI lab that released a series of open-weights models — most famously the R1 reasoning model — that matched frontier performance at a fraction of the typical training cost, shaking the industry in early 2025. 'Open weights' means the model files are publicly downloadable: anyone can run, fine-tune, or inspect them, unlike closed models that only exist behind an API." },
        { title: 'Why open weights matter', text: "Open models change the power dynamics: you can self-host (privacy, no per-token fees after hardware), fine-tune for specialized domains, audit behavior, and avoid vendor lock-in. The trade-offs: you carry the infrastructure burden, safety guardrails are thinner, and hosted versions still route data through the provider — open weights helps only if you run it yourself." },
        { title: 'Reasoning models', text: "DeepSeek-R1 is a reasoning model: it generates an explicit chain-of-thought before answering, spending more compute to think harder on math, logic, and multi-step problems. The visible reasoning trace is itself a feature — you can watch where the logic goes wrong instead of just grading the final answer." },
        { title: 'Access paths', text: "Two ways in: the consumer apps (deepseek.com, mobile) for chat and reasoning mode, and the API (api-docs.deepseek.com) for developers — notably cheap per token versus frontier alternatives. This course uses the app for the main lessons and touches the API in the developer lesson." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Open vs closed worksheet', instructions: ['Write the difference between open-weights and closed models in two sentences.', 'List one advantage and one responsibility self-hosting brings.', 'Open DeepSeek and locate the reasoning mode toggle.', 'Note one task from your week where a reasoning model could help.'], solution: 'Open weights: model files are downloadable so you can run/modify them yourself; closed models are only accessible via provider APIs. Advantage: privacy and no per-call cost; responsibility: you handle hardware, updates, and safety. Reasoning tasks worth trying: multi-step analysis, math, code debugging, logic puzzles.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What does “open weights” mean?', options: ['The model is free to use online', 'The model’s parameter files are publicly downloadable — you can run and modify it yourself', 'The source code for the chat app is public'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Weights are the trained parameters. Open weights means the model itself is downloadable — enabling self-hosting, fine-tuning, and auditing that closed APIs cannot offer.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Explain reasoning mode vs chat mode.', 'Decide when reasoning is worth the latency.', 'Read a reasoning trace critically.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Two modes, two jobs', text: "DeepSeek offers chat mode (fast, direct answers like a standard assistant) and reasoning mode (the model thinks step by step first, then answers). Reasoning produces dramatically better results on math, logic, code, and multi-step analysis — at the cost of visible delay while it 'thinks'. Using chat mode for hard problems and reasoning mode for 'what is the capital of France' are both mistakes." },
        { title: 'When the thinking is worth it', text: "Rule of thumb: if you could solve it with a quick lookup, chat mode; if it requires connecting multiple steps — debugging logic, math proofs, weighing options, planning — reasoning mode. The reasoning trace also builds trust: seeing the steps lets you catch where the model took a wrong turn rather than trusting a confident-sounding conclusion." },
        { title: 'Reading the trace', text: "The visible chain-of-thought is a diagnostic tool, not gospel. Watch for: skipped steps (jumps to conclusions), circular reasoning (restating the problem as progress), and self-correction loops that never resolve. A clean trace with a checkable final step is good evidence; a meandering trace producing a slick answer is a red flag to verify." },
        { title: 'Prompting for reasoning', text: "Reasoning models respond to well-posed problems: state the goal precisely, include all relevant inputs, and let the model think — elaborate step-by-step instructions can actually hurt because the model's own reasoning is the point. Ask it to verify its answer or check edge cases afterward; self-checking prompts pair naturally with reasoning models." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Reasoning drill', instructions: ['Take a multi-step problem (a logic puzzle, a math word problem, or a debugging scenario).', 'Ask it in chat mode; note the answer and confidence.', 'Ask the same problem in reasoning mode; read the trace.', 'Compare: which answer was better, and what did the trace reveal?'], solution: 'On genuine multi-step problems, reasoning mode should produce more reliable answers with an inspectable trace. Note where the trace verified vs guessed — that comparison is the skill: knowing when to spend reasoning-mode latency.' },
      quiz: { question: 'When should you use DeepSeek’s reasoning mode?', options: ['For every question', 'For multi-step problems where the reasoning process itself matters — math, logic, debugging', 'Only for code'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Reasoning spends compute and time thinking through steps — worth it for hard multi-step problems, wasteful for simple lookups.' },
    },
  ],
  'kimi-in-practice': [
    {
      objectives: ['Describe Kimi and Moonshot AI.', 'Explain the long-context advantage.', 'Access Kimi and its core interface.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Kimi and Moonshot', text: "Kimi is the consumer assistant from Moonshot AI, a Chinese lab that bet early on extreme context length — holding millions of tokens (entire books, hundreds of pages of documents) in working memory where competitors capped out. That made Kimi a breakout product in China for research, legal, and academic document work, and its newer K2 models added agentic capabilities on top." },
        { title: 'Why context length is the feature', text: "Most assistants handle a few dozen pages well. Kimi's specialty is ingesting entire document sets — contracts, case files, literature reviews — and answering questions that span all of them at once. The workflow difference is qualitative: instead of chunking a 500-page report into pieces and reconciling summaries yourself, you ask the question across the whole thing." },
        { title: 'Where Kimi fits', text: "Kimi's sweet spot: research-heavy knowledge work — literature reviews, due diligence, regulatory filings, competitive analysis — where the bottleneck is reading volume. Its bilingual Chinese-English strength also makes it the natural choice when source materials span both languages. For casual chat or creative writing, any assistant works; for document-scale analysis, Kimi earns its place." },
        { title: 'Getting started', text: "Sign up at kimi.com (international) or kimi.moonshot.cn. The interface centers on the chat plus document upload — the upload button is where the product lives. Free tier covers meaningful document work; heavier use and agent features sit behind limits or paid tiers. Start by uploading something genuinely long — that is the whole point." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Context audit', instructions: ['Find the longest document you work with regularly — count its pages.', 'List three questions you could ask across that document plus two related files.', 'Create a Kimi account and upload a document.', 'Ask one of your questions and note whether the answer cites locations in the text.'], solution: 'The exercise exposes the fit: if your work involves long documents and cross-file questions, Kimi’s context is the unlock. A good first answer should reference specific sections — verify at least one citation against the source to start building verification habits.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What is Kimi’s signature capability?', options: ['Image generation', 'Extremely long context — analyzing entire books and document sets in one pass', 'Real-time translation'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Kimi built its product around context windows measured in millions of tokens, enabling questions across whole document sets rather than chunked excerpts.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Upload documents to Kimi effectively.', 'Ask targeted questions about document content.', 'Interpret answers with source awareness.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Uploads that work', text: "Kimi accepts common formats — PDF, Word, slides, spreadsheets, images of text. Quality matters: text-based PDFs work cleanly; scanned documents depend on OCR quality; very image-heavy files lose nuance. For best results, upload clean digital originals when you have them, and name files clearly — the model uses filenames as context for multi-document questions." },
        { title: 'Questions that extract value', text: "Document questions work on a spectrum: locate ('find the termination clause'), extract ('list every payment deadline with amounts'), summarize ('five-bullet summary of section 3'), and analyze ('what risks does this contract create for the seller?'). The extract and analyze tiers are where long context pays — ask questions that require holding the whole document, not just keyword search." },
        { title: 'Citations and source awareness', text: "Good document answers reference where in the text claims come from — page or section. Kimi often surfaces these; when it does not, ask for them explicitly ('cite the section for each claim'). This is not bureaucratic fuss — it is your verification path. Treat any uncited claim as a draft to confirm, especially for legal, financial, or compliance material." },
        { title: 'Multi-file sessions', text: "The real power move: upload several related files — a contract plus its amendments, three competitor reports, a study and its rebuttal — and ask cross-document questions: 'where do these agreements conflict?' or 'what do all three reports agree on?' Cross-referencing across a set is exactly what chunking workflows could never do." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Document drill', instructions: ['Upload a real document (report, paper, contract) to Kimi.', 'Ask a locate question, an extract question, and an analyze question.', 'For the extract answer, verify two items against the source.', 'Write the one question you would ask this document again.'], solution: 'Extract questions should return structured answers with locatable sources — verify them. Analyze questions produce interpretation — valuable but requiring judgment. The repeatable pattern: locate → extract → analyze, verifying at each tier before trusting the next.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Why ask Kimi to cite document sections in its answers?', options: ['It looks professional', 'Citations are your verification path — uncited claims need manual confirmation against the source', 'It makes the answer longer'], answer: 1, explanation: 'On document work, the answer is only as good as your ability to check it. Explicit citations turn a plausible summary into verifiable claims.' },
    },
  ],
  'glm-in-practice': [
    {
      objectives: ['Describe GLM and Zhipu AI.', 'Access ChatGLM and the open platform.', 'Identify GLM’s bilingual and open-weight strengths.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'GLM and Zhipu AI', text: "GLM (General Language Model) is the model family from Zhipu AI — a major Chinese lab spun out of Tsinghua University. The ChatGLM series built the domestic following; GLM-4 brought frontier-competitive capability; and GLM-4.5/4.6 added agent and coding strengths. Unusually among major labs, Zhipu releases significant open-weights models alongside its commercial API." },
        { title: 'The bilingual edge', text: "GLM models are trained bilingually from the start — Chinese and English as equals rather than translation layers. For work spanning Chinese and English materials — sourcing, translation, cross-language research — they often match or beat Western frontier models, while pure-English tasks put them in a competitive-but-not-dominant pack." },
        { title: 'Open weights and self-hosting', text: "Like DeepSeek, Zhipu publishes open GLM weights (ChatGLM series, GLM-4.x open variants) — downloadable, runnable locally, fine-tunable. For organizations in regulated industries or with data-residency requirements, a self-hosted GLM offers capable multilingual performance without data leaving your infrastructure — the same open-weights trade-off calculus as DeepSeek." },
        { title: 'Access paths', text: "Consumer access via chatglm.cn and the international app; developer access via the Zhipu open platform (open.bigmodel.cn) with an API compatible with familiar patterns. This course uses the apps for daily workflows and touches the API in the developer lesson — the goal is knowing when GLM belongs in your toolkit." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Bilingual fit check', instructions: ['Write one sentence on how much of your work involves Chinese content or speakers.', 'List two tasks where a bilingual-first model could help.', 'Open ChatGLM or the Zhipu platform and locate the main features.', 'Note one open-weights use case relevant to your situation.'], solution: 'If your work touches Chinese-language materials, customers, or research, GLM earns a slot in the toolkit; otherwise it is a strong open alternative worth knowing. Open-weights relevance: self-hosting for privacy, fine-tuning for domain, or cost control at scale.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What distinguishes GLM among global assistants?', options: ['It only works in Chinese', 'Bilingual-first training (Chinese/English as equals) plus significant open-weights releases', 'It has no API'], answer: 1, explanation: 'GLM’s edge is genuine bilingual capability from training, plus a serious open-weights program enabling self-hosting — a combination few Western labs offer.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Set up GLM access and interface.', 'Write effective first prompts.', 'Test GLM on everyday tasks.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Getting oriented', text: "ChatGLM's interface follows the familiar assistant pattern: chat input, file upload, and mode options depending on tier. For international users the web app or API is the cleanest entry; Chinese users get the full domestic feature set. Create an account, find the upload button, and identify which model variants are available on your tier — naming differs across GLM-4.x versions." },
        { title: 'First prompts that work', text: "GLM responds to the same prompting discipline as every assistant — context, task, constraints — with one addition: it handles bilingual prompts natively, so you can ask in English about Chinese documents or vice versa without ceremony. For a first real test, give it a task with a checkable output: summarize an article, extract a table, translate a paragraph you can evaluate." },
        { title: 'Bilingual prompting', text: "The bilingual superpower in practice: paste a Chinese document and ask English questions, or draft in English and request natural Chinese output. Quality is strongest in Chinese↔English pairs. Watch for the usual caveats — specialized terminology still needs human review, and idiomatic phrasing benefits from an explicit 'natural, not literal' instruction." },
        { title: 'Calibrating expectations', text: "Honest positioning: GLM is competitive for everyday tasks and excellent for bilingual work, but for the hardest English-only reasoning, frontier models still often edge ahead. Test it on YOUR tasks rather than benchmarks — a model that handles your weekly bilingual reports well beats one that wins leaderboards on tests you never run." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'First-task benchmark', instructions: ['Pick one everyday task (summarize an article, draft an email, extract data).', 'Run it on GLM with a clear context-task-constraints prompt.', 'Run the identical prompt on your usual assistant.', 'Score both outputs for accuracy, usefulness, and style in two sentences.'], solution: 'The point is a personal benchmark, not a verdict: GLM should handle standard tasks well and shine on bilingual ones. Note specific wins/losses — those observations drive which assistant you reach for per task later.' },
      quiz: { question: 'When does GLM most clearly beat Western frontier models?', options: ['On all tasks uniformly', 'On Chinese-English bilingual work — it was trained for both languages natively', 'On image generation'], answer: 1, explanation: 'GLM’s clearest advantage is bilingual fluency — for pure English tasks it is competitive rather than dominant, so choose it where its strength applies.' },
    },
  ],
  'devin-for-developers': [
    {
      objectives: ['Explain what an AI software engineer does differently from a chat assistant.', 'Describe Devin’s workflow: brief, plan, execute, review.', 'Set expectations for what agents can and cannot do.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Agents vs assistants', text: "Chat assistants answer; agents act. Devin — Cognition's AI software engineer — does not just suggest code in a chat window: it takes a task, explores a real repository, writes code, runs tests, fixes its own errors, and produces a finished result (often a pull request) with a transcript of everything it did. You manage it like a junior engineer: brief it, review the output, iterate." },
        { title: 'The Devin workflow', text: "A typical task runs: you write a brief (goal + acceptance criteria) → Devin plans and asks clarifying questions → it works in its own environment with shell, editor, and browser → you review the delivered diff and logs → you request changes or merge. The leverage comes from running multiple tasks in parallel while you review rather than write." },
        { title: 'What it is good at today', text: "Strong: scoped bug fixes, dependency upgrades, test writing, migrations, small features with clear acceptance criteria, documentation updates, codebase Q&A via its exploration tools. Weak: ambiguous product decisions, large architectural changes, tasks needing your unstated context, anything where 'done' is a judgment call only you can make. Match the task to the capability and results are striking; mismatch them and you get expensive churn." },
        { title: 'The management mindset', text: "The skill this course teaches is delegation, not prompting magic: writing briefs an engineer (human or AI) can execute without guessing, reviewing output systematically, and giving feedback that converges. If you have ever onboarded a junior dev or outsourced work, the instincts transfer — Devin is tireless, fast, and literal, which amplifies both good briefs and vague ones." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Delegation audit', instructions: ['List five tasks from your backlog or a side project.', 'Mark each delegate-able or do-it-yourself using the criteria above.', 'For one delegate-able task, draft a two-sentence brief.', 'Note what context Devin would need that you have in your head.'], solution: 'Good candidates: fix bug X with repro steps, upgrade library Y and update usages, add tests for module Z, migrate config format. Poor candidates: redesign the architecture, figure out why users churn, make it faster somehow. The brief should name the goal, the relevant files, and how to verify completion — the context gap is what good briefs close.' },
      quiz: { question: 'How does Devin differ from a chat coding assistant?', options: ['It writes better code', 'It executes tasks autonomously — exploring repos, running tests, and delivering finished work — rather than only suggesting text', 'It does not need supervision'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The difference is agency: Devin acts in a real environment end to end. You delegate and review instead of copy-pasting suggestions — and it still needs good briefs and review.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Identify task shapes agents complete reliably.', 'Write acceptance criteria an agent can verify.', 'Decompose oversized tasks.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'The completable task shape', text: "Tasks agents finish share a shape: clear goal, bounded scope, verifiable done. 'Fix the login redirect loop after OAuth — reproduce with these steps, add a test covering it' is completable. 'Make auth better' is not. The test is whether a competent engineer could tell when the task is finished without asking you — write briefs that pass that bar." },
        { title: 'Acceptance criteria are the contract', text: "The single highest-leverage habit: state done-ness explicitly. 'The fix should make X pass, not break Y, and keep Z behavior' gives the agent a target to self-verify against — and Devin does run tests and iterate toward them. Without criteria, the agent guesses at your intent; with them, it converges. Include the verification command if you know it ('npm test should pass')." },
        { title: 'Context beats cleverness', text: "Agents fail on missing context more than on hard code. Point to the relevant files, link the error message, mention the gotcha ('the tests need the DB seeded'), name the conventions to follow. Thirty seconds of pointer-dropping in the brief saves an hour of the agent rediscovering what you already knew — and the wrong paths it explores otherwise." },
        { title: 'Splitting oversized tasks', text: "When a task is too big, decompose it the way you would for a junior hire: separate PRs for separate concerns, land a scaffold/refactor first, then the feature. 'Migrate everything to TypeScript' becomes 'convert utils/ to TypeScript' → 'convert models/' → etc. Small sequential tasks with independent verification beat one heroic vague task every time." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Write a completable brief', instructions: ['Take a real task from your work.', 'Write a brief: goal, relevant files, gotchas, acceptance criteria, verification command.', 'Check it against the completable shape — can a stranger tell when it is done?', 'If it is too big, split it into two sequenced tasks.'], solution: 'A passing brief looks like: “In api/auth.js, the OAuth callback redirects to / instead of the session URL when state is missing — fix to fall back to /dashboard. Add a test in auth.test.js covering missing-state. Accept: npm test passes; manual repro steps in the issue no longer loop.” Weak briefs lack the accept criteria or file pointers.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What makes a task brief completable for an agent?', options: ['Being very short', 'Clear goal + bounded scope + explicit acceptance criteria the agent can verify', 'Using technical jargon'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Agents converge when done-ness is checkable. Acceptance criteria turn “improve X” into a verifiable target the agent can test itself against.' },
    },
  ],
  'jev-system-one-models': [
    {
      objectives: ['Explain what a System One model does.', 'Distinguish decision models from text generators.', 'Identify automation tasks that need decisions, not prose.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Built for automation, not conversation', text: "Chat models are superhuman at generating text — and that is exactly the problem for automation: software needs a decision it can act on, not a paragraph a human must parse. TypeSafe AI's System One models are a different class: you send unstructured input (text, program state, a question) and get back a typed, structured decision with calibrated probabilities — a frontier-intelligence function call, not a chatbot." },
        { title: 'Typed output, not tokens', text: "Jev does not generate strings at all. You define the output schema up front — a classification into fixed labels, a score in a range, a set of extracted fields — and the model fills it with probabilities for every option, sampled in parallel in a single query. No parsing, no type errors, no 'unexpected format' bugs: the output is always machine-usable by construction.", code: "input:   unstructured state (ticket text, user record, doc)\noutput:  { intent: {billing:0.71, bug:0.18, other:0.11},\n           urgency: {high:0.62, med:0.30, low:0.08} }\nevery field typed, probabilities calibrated", language: 'text' },
        { title: 'Calibrated confidence', text: "Every answer carries calibrated probabilities — when Jev says 0.9 it is right about 90% of the time, unlike LLM 'confidence' which is text about confidence rather than a measure of it. This is the property that makes automation possible: you can branch on thresholds ('only auto-route above 0.8') and trust that the threshold means what it says." },
        { title: 'Where it fits', text: "System One models target the gap where LLMs are awkward: smart if-statements inside code (classify, route, score, extract, branch), map-reducing over big data, and verifying other models' outputs. They are ~100x faster and far cheaper per call precisely because they skip string generation — the trade is no prose, no conversation, no code output: decisions only." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Decision-task inventory', instructions: ['List five tasks in software you know that currently need a human judgment call.', 'For each, write the decision as a typed output (labels, scores, or fields).', 'Mark which are bottlenecked by needing text parsed vs needing a decision.', 'Pick the one where a calibrated probability would most change the design.'], solution: 'Good candidates: ticket routing ({category, urgency}), content moderation ({label, confidence}), extraction ({fields with scores}), eligibility checks ({decision, probability}). The best pick is the one where a probability threshold, not a text answer, drives the next step.' },
      quiz: { question: 'What does a System One model output?', options: ['Generated text like an LLM', 'Typed, structured decisions with calibrated probabilities — no strings at all', 'Code completions'], answer: 1, explanation: 'System One models skip string generation entirely: outputs are schema-typed values with probabilities, designed for software to consume directly.' },
    },
    {
      objectives: ['Compare LLM text generation with System One decisions.', 'Explain the parallel sampling advantage.', 'Match the model type to the task shape.'],
      sections: [
        { title: 'Two different frontiers', text: "LLMs optimize for human-preferred text via RLHF — great for chat, copilots, and generation, but their output is a string your code must parse, validate, and hope stays in format. System One models optimize for calibrated decisions: the output space is defined by your schema, probabilities come with it, and there is nothing to parse. They are complements, not competitors — generation vs decision." },
        { title: 'Why it is fast', text: "LLMs generate token-by-token, each conditioned on the last — sequential by nature, seconds per response. Jev samples all outputs in parallel in a single query, which is why response times run 70-500ms instead of 3-300s. For anything inside a request path or a tight loop, that difference is the difference between usable and unusable." },
        { title: 'No hallucination — by construction', text: "Hallucination is a property of free-form generation: the model invents plausible text. Jev cannot produce anything outside your defined schema — there is no string space to hallucinate into. The failure mode shifts instead: it can assign the wrong probability to a valid label (a decision error), which calibrated confidence surfaces rather than hides." },
        { title: 'Choosing the tool', text: "Use an LLM when the deliverable is text for a human: drafts, explanations, code, conversation. Use a System One model when the deliverable is a decision for software: route this, score that, extract these fields, verify the other model's output. Many production systems need both — the LLM writes the reply, Jev decides whether to send it." },
      ],
      exercise: { title: 'Task-shape matching', instructions: ['Classify each as LLM-task or System-One-task: draft a support reply; decide if a ticket is billing-related; summarize a doc; score a lead 0-1; write a function; guardrail an LLM answer.', 'For one System-One task, write the output schema.', 'Write one workflow where both models appear.', 'Note what Jev cannot do in your list.'], solution: 'LLM: reply, summary, function. System One: ticket decision, lead score, guardrail. Schema example: {isBilling: probability, confidence, reason-class}. Combined workflow: Jev classifies ticket → LLM drafts reply → Jev scores reply quality → send or escalate. Jev cannot generate any text — that is the trade.' },
      quiz: { question: 'Why can Jev not hallucinate?', options: ['It is a smaller model', 'There is no free-form string space — output is constrained to your typed schema, so invention is impossible by construction', 'It uses RAG'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Hallucination lives in open-ended generation. Schema-typed output removes the space to hallucinate into — the remaining risk is wrong probabilities, which calibration exposes.' },
    },
  ],
};

